// Sections 68-82: typeclasses.

render_section_68 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A typeclass is a named contract over one or more type parameters. It is
        \\introduced by an ordinary constant declaration whose value is a
        \\`typeclass` body, so the form is `Name :: typeclass (P: kind, ...) { ... }`.
        \\The body lists requirements: methods that an instance must supply and
        \\associated types that an instance must choose. A class declares no
        \\representation and produces no runtime value of its own; it fixes a set of
        \\names and signatures that instances then fill in.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Describable :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(describe) {
        \\    describe :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Port :: struct { number: s64; }
        \\
        \\Port :: instance Describable {
        \\    describe :: (value: Port) -> string { return "port " + show(value.number); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Port = .{ .number = 80 };
        \\    print("%\n", describe(p));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\port 80
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Class names live in a single global namespace, and so do method names.
        \\Re-declaring a name the standard library already uses is
        \\`error: typeclass 'Show' is already declared`, and declaring a method whose
        \\name another class owns is
        \\`error: function 'show' is already owned by typeclass 'Show'`. The second
        \\rule applies between two user classes as well, so a class body may not
        \\reuse `show`, `fmap`, `bind`, or any other name already claimed. The reason
        \\is dispatch: a method call is resolved by name across every instance in the
        \\program, so one name can belong to only one contract.
    );
    subheading(4, "The declaration header");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The header between `typeclass` and the opening brace has a fixed clause
        \\order. Everything after the parameter list is optional, but a `where`
        \\clause may not follow a directive: `#minimal(f) where Base(T)` is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'where'`.
    );
    table_header(6, "Clause   —   Count   —   Position");
    table_row(7, "`(P: kind, ...)`   |   exactly one   |   immediately after `typeclass`");
    table_row(8, "`where <expr>`   |   zero or more   |   after the parameter list");
    table_row(9, "`#derive(macro, ...)`   |   zero or more   |   after the `where` clauses");
    table_row(10, "`#minimal(formula)`   |   at most one   |   after the `where` clauses");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\A trailing comma is allowed in the parameter list. `#derive` may appear
        \\more than once; the macro it names is what generates an instance when a
        \\type asks for one, which is the subject of Deriving Instances with
        \\`#derive`. A second `#minimal` is
        \\`error: duplicate #minimal directive on typeclass 'K'`.
    );
    subheading(12, "Parameter kinds");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\A parameter is written as an ordinary annotated binding, and the annotation
        \\is its kind. Two spellings carry meaning: `type` for a parameter that binds
        \\a type, and an arrow for a parameter that binds a type constructor. The
        \\number of parameters decides the shape of every instance head for the
        \\class.
    );
    table_header(14, "Kind   —   Binds   —   Standard-library example");
    table_row(15, "`T: type`   |   one type   |   `Show(T)`, `Iterable(T)`, `Try(Carrier)`");
    table_row(16, "`L: type, R: type`   |   a pair of types   |   `Add(L, R)`, `PartialEq(L, R)`");
    table_row(17, "`F: (type) -> type`   |   a one-argument type constructor   |   `Functor(F)`, `Monad(M)`");
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\// one type parameter
        \\Tagged :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(tag_of) {
        \\    tag_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\// two type parameters
        \\Blend :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) #minimal(blend) {
        \\    Output :: type;
        \\    blend :: (left: L, right: R) -> Output;
        \\}
        \\
        \\// a type-constructor parameter
        \\Holder :: typeclass (F: (type) -> type) #minimal(peek) {
        \\    peek :: (container: F($A)) -> A;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Cell :: struct { value: $A; }
        \\
        \\s64 :: instance Tagged { tag_of :: (value: s64) -> string { return "int"; } }
        \\
        \\(s64, string) :: instance Blend {
        \\    Output :: string;
        \\    blend :: (left: s64, right: string) -> string { return show(left) + right; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Cell :: instance Holder {
        \\    peek :: (container: Cell($A)) -> A { return container.value; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: s64 = 3;
        \\    c: Cell(string) = .{ .value = "inside" };
        \\    print("%\n", tag_of(n));
        \\    print("%\n", blend(7, "x"));
        \\    print("%\n", peek(c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\int
        \\7x
        \\inside
    );
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\The three heads differ because the classes do. A one-parameter class takes
        \\a single type; a two-parameter class takes a parenthesised argument tuple;
        \\a constructor parameter takes the bare constructor name, with evidence
        \\asked at the applied type. Arrows of higher arity are accepted, so
        \\`(type, type) -> type` binds a two-argument constructor. An annotation that
        \\is neither spelling parses, but the parameter still binds a type: there are
        \\no value parameters on a class. Instance heads are the subject of
        \\Instances: Heads, Bodies, and `where`, and constructor parameters of
        \\Higher-Kinded Typeclasses.
    );
    note(21, #string,oneline
        \\A class declared with an empty parameter list parses, but it can never
        \\acquire evidence, because an evidence query is a question about a type
        \\argument and there is nowhere to put one. Its instance methods fail that
        \\check at every call, so `ping(n)` is
        \\`error: no overload 'ping' accepts these arguments` even when an instance
        \\declares exactly that signature. Give every class at least one parameter.
    );
    subheading(22, "Methods and associated types");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\A class body holds two kinds of member. A member whose value starts with a
        \\parenthesis is a method; any other member is an associated type. The
        \\distinction is made at parse time, which is why there are no associated
        \\constants: a non-parenthesised member is read with the type parser.
    );
    table_header(24, "Member   —   Meaning");
    table_row(25, "`Name :: <type-expression>;`   |   an associated type an instance must choose");
    table_row(26, "`name :: (params) -> Ret;`   |   a method requirement with no default");
    table_row(27, "`name :: (params) -> Ret { ... }`   |   a method requirement with a default body");
    table_row(28, "`'op' :: (params) -> Ret #operator(...);`   |   an operator requirement");
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Describable :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(describe) {
        \\    describe :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\    describe_twice :: (value: T) -> string {
        \\        return describe(value) + " / " + describe(value);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Port :: struct { number: s64; }
        \\Host :: struct { name: string; }
        \\
        \\Port :: instance Describable {
        \\    describe :: (value: Port) -> string { return "port " + show(value.number); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Host :: instance Describable {
        \\    describe :: (value: Host) -> string { return "host " + value.name; }
        \\    describe_twice :: (value: Host) -> string { return "host pair"; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\label_for :: (value: $T) -> string where Describable(T) {
        \\    return describe_twice(value);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Port = .{ .number = 80 };
        \\    h: Host = .{ .name = "local" };
        \\    print("%\n", label_for(p));
        \\    print("%\n", label_for(h));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\port 80 / port 80
        \\host pair
    );
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\`Port` inherits the default `describe_twice`; `Host` replaces it. An
        \\instance method always beats a class default, because selection drops every
        \\default candidate as soon as one instance candidate has matched. A default
        \\body is therefore a fallback, never a preference. The class parameters are
        \\in scope through the whole body as plain names, and writing one of them
        \\again as a generic is
        \\`error: typeclass parameter 'T' is already declared; use plain T inside the typeclass method`.
    );
    subheading(32, "Superclass constraints");
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\A `where` clause on the class declaration is a superclass constraint: it is
        \\a condition on the class parameters that must hold before any instance of
        \\the class counts. The clauses are evaluated on every evidence query, ahead
        \\of the instance list.
    );
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Weighted :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(weight_of) {
        \\    weight_of :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Ranked :: typeclass (T: type) where Weighted(T) #minimal(rank_of) {
        \\    rank_of :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Crate :: struct { kilos: s64; }
        \\Crate :: instance Weighted { weight_of :: (value: Crate) -> s64 { return value.kilos; } }
        \\Crate :: instance Ranked   { rank_of   :: (value: Crate) -> s64 { return 1; } }
        \\
        \\Balloon :: struct { kilos: s64; }
        \\Balloon :: instance Ranked { rank_of :: (value: Balloon) -> s64 { return 2; } }
        \\
        \\RANKED_CRATE   :: Ranked(Crate);
        \\RANKED_BALLOON :: Ranked(Balloon);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: Crate = .{ .kilos = 5 };
        \\    print("Ranked(Crate)=% Ranked(Balloon)=%\n", RANKED_CRATE, RANKED_BALLOON);
        \\    print("rank %\n", rank_of(c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(35, "text", #string
        \\Ranked(Crate)=1 Ranked(Balloon)=0
        \\rank 1
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\The `Balloon` instance is accepted at its declaration: a superclass clause
        \\is not checked when an instance is written, only when the class is
        \\consulted. The declaration is simply inert. Every query answers `0` and
        \\every call is rejected, so calling `rank_of` on a `Balloon` in the program
        \\above is `error: no overload 'rank_of' accepts these arguments`. Supplying
        \\the missing `Weighted` instance revives the `Ranked` one with no other
        \\edit.
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\The standard library uses this to layer contracts:
        \\`Eq :: typeclass (T: type) where PartialEq(T, T) {}` is a marker over
        \\structural equality, `Applicative` requires `Functor`, `Monad` requires
        \\`Applicative`, and `Fractional` requires `Num(T) && Div(T, T)`. A single
        \\clause may join conditions with `&&`, and several clauses may be chained;
        \\all of them are conjoined.
    );
    subheading(38, "What a class declaration emits");
    bullet(39, #string,oneline
        \\A compile-time predicate under the class's own name. `Describable(Port)` is
        \\a constant boolean, described in Evidence, Constraints, and Negative
        \\Instances.
    );
    bullet(40, #string,oneline
        \\One globally owned overload set per method name. Instance methods and class
        \\defaults are candidates in that set; nothing else may declare the name.
    );
    bullet(41, #string,oneline
        \\One projectable name per associated type, written `Class(args).Name` in
        \\type position and covered in Associated Types.
    );
    bullet(42, #string,oneline
        \\For an operator member, the spelling, arity, fixity and precedence, all
        \\registered globally. The class alone makes `a <*> b` parse; only the
        \\instance is missing, and the call is `error: no operator '<*>' for int, int`
        \\until one exists. The Operator System covers the rules.
    );
}

render_section_69 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`#minimal` states the smallest set of members an instance must supply for
        \\the class to consider it complete. Members outside that set are expected to
        \\have default bodies that are written in terms of the members inside it, so
        \\the directive is what makes a class with several mutually defined methods
        \\usable: an instance picks whichever member it can implement directly and
        \\the rest follow.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Signed_Pair :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(low | high) {
        \\    low  :: (value: T) -> s64 { return -high(value); }
        \\    high :: (value: T) -> s64 { return -low(value); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Floor :: struct {}
        \\Ceil  :: struct {}
        \\
        \\Floor :: instance Signed_Pair { low  :: (value: Floor) -> s64 { return 5; } }
        \\Ceil  :: instance Signed_Pair { high :: (value: Ceil)  -> s64 { return 9; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    f: Floor;
        \\    c: Ceil;
        \\    print("Floor low=% high=%\n", low(f), high(f));
        \\    print("Ceil  low=% high=%\n", low(c), high(c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\Floor low=5 high=-5
        \\Ceil  low=-9 high=9
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Both members carry default bodies, so without the directive neither would
        \\be required, an empty instance would be accepted, and `low` and `high`
        \\would call each other forever. `#minimal(low | high)` states the real
        \\obligation: implement one of the two, and the other is derived.
    );
    subheading(4, "The formula grammar");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The formula is a small propositional language over method names. It is
        \\parsed by a dedicated parser, not by the expression parser, so the operator
        \\table plays no part and the spellings below are the whole vocabulary.
    );
    code_block(6, "ebnf", #string
        \\minimal     = disjunction ;
        \\disjunction = conjunction { "|" conjunction } ;
        \\conjunction = atom { "," atom } ;
        \\atom        = method-name | quoted-operator | "(" disjunction ")" ;
    );
    table_header(7, "Form   —   Meaning");
    table_row(8, "`name`   |   the method `name` is implemented");
    table_row(9, "`'op'`   |   the operator member `op` is implemented");
    table_row(10, "`A, B`   |   both `A` and `B`");
    table_row(11, "`A | B`   |   either `A` or `B`");
    table_row(12, "`( ... )`   |   grouping; empty parentheses are an error");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\Conjunction binds tighter than disjunction, so `a, b | c` means
        \\`(a, b) | c`. Parentheses are needed only for the other reading. An empty
        \\group is `error: #minimal parentheses cannot be empty`, and a dangling
        \\separator is `error: expected a method name after ',' in #minimal` or the
        \\matching message for `|`. A name that is not a method of the class is
        \\`error: #minimal references unknown typeclass method nope`.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Split :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal((head_of, tail_of) | whole_of) {
        \\    head_of  :: (value: T) -> s64 { return whole_of(value) / 10; }
        \\    tail_of  :: (value: T) -> s64 { return whole_of(value) % 10; }
        \\    whole_of :: (value: T) -> s64 { return head_of(value) * 10 + tail_of(value); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Joined :: struct {}
        \\Parted :: struct {}
        \\
        \\Joined :: instance Split { whole_of :: (value: Joined) -> s64 { return 47; } }
        \\Parted :: instance Split {
        \\    head_of :: (value: Parted) -> s64 { return 1; }
        \\    tail_of :: (value: Parted) -> s64 { return 2; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    j: Joined;
        \\    p: Parted;
        \\    print("Joined head=% tail=% whole=%\n", head_of(j), tail_of(j), whole_of(j));
        \\    print("Parted head=% tail=% whole=%\n", head_of(p), tail_of(p), whole_of(p));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\Joined head=4 tail=7 whole=47
        \\Parted head=1 tail=2 whole=12
    );
    subheading(16, "Operator atoms");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\An operator member is named in a formula with its quoted spelling, the same
        \\form used to declare it. Quoting is required: the formula parser reads an
        \\unquoted operator token as punctuation, and `#minimal(+)` is
        \\`error: operators in #minimal must use quoted operator names`.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Nearby :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) #minimal('~=' | '!~') {
        \\    '~=' :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool #operator(left, 8) { return !(a !~ b); }
        \\    '!~' :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool #operator(left, 8) { return !(a ~= b); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Reading :: struct { value: s64; }
        \\
        \\(Reading, Reading) :: instance Nearby {
        \\    '~=' :: (a: Reading, b: Reading) -> bool {
        \\        d := a.value - b.value;
        \\        if d < 0 d = -d;
        \\        return d <= 2;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    x: Reading = .{ .value = 10 };
        \\    y: Reading = .{ .value = 11 };
        \\    z: Reading = .{ .value = 20 };
        \\    print("x ~= y  %\n", x ~= y);
        \\    print("x !~ z  %\n", x !~ z);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\x ~= y  1
        \\x !~ z  1
    );
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\This is exactly the shape of the Prelude's `PartialEq`, which is declared
        \\`#minimal('==' | '!=')` with each operator defaulting to the negation of
        \\the other. An unsatisfied operator formula reports the quoted names back:
        \\`error: instance '(Z, Z) :: Nearby' does not satisfy #minimal('~=' | '!~')`.
    );
    subheading(21, "The empty formula and the implicit minimal");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`#minimal()` requires nothing. It marks a class whose purpose is the
        \\evidence itself rather than any method, and it is what lets an instance
        \\body be empty even when the class declares members.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Trusted :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal() {
        \\    audit :: (value: T) -> s64 { return 0; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Ledger :: struct {}
        \\Ledger :: instance Trusted {}
        \\
        \\TRUSTED_LEDGER :: Trusted(Ledger);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    l: Ledger;
        \\    print("audit=% evidence=%\n", audit(l), TRUSTED_LEDGER);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", #string
        \\audit=0 evidence=1
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\Omitting the directive is not the same as writing `#minimal()`. With no
        \\directive the class gets an implicit minimal: every method requirement that
        \\has no default body must be implemented. That is the common case and the
        \\reason most classes need no directive at all. Its failure has its own
        \\wording, and it names the members that are missing.
    );
    code_block(26, "glosso", #string
        \\Pairish :: typeclass (T: type) {
        \\    first  :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\    second :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\    both   :: (value: T) -> s64 { return first(value) + second(value); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Empty :: struct {}
        \\Empty :: instance Pairish {}
        \\
        \\main :: () {}
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\error: instance 'Empty :: Pairish' does not satisfy the implicit minimal definition; missing first, second
        \\  --> minimal.glo:8:1
        \\  |
        \\8 | Empty :: instance Pairish {}
        \\  | ^^^^^
    );
    subheading(28, "How satisfaction is decided");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\The check runs at declaration time, once per instance group, before any
        \\call is resolved. For each member the class requires, the compiler builds a
        \\signature key from the name, the arity, the fixity, and the parameter and
        \\return annotations with the head arguments and the instance's associated
        \\types substituted in; a supplied method satisfies a requirement when the
        \\two keys agree. The formula is then evaluated over the requirements that
        \\were matched.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\Bounded :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(least, greatest) {
        \\    least    :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\    greatest :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Span :: struct {}
        \\Span :: instance Bounded {
        \\    least :: (value: Span) -> s64 { return 1; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\error: instance 'Span :: Bounded' does not satisfy #minimal(least, greatest)
        \\  --> formula.glo:7:1
        \\  |
        \\7 | Span :: instance Bounded {
        \\  | ^^^^
        \\note: minimal complete definition declared here
        \\  --> formula.glo:1:32
        \\  |
        \\1 | Bounded :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(least, greatest) {
        \\  |                                ^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Because the comparison is on whole signature keys, a method that is present
        \\but wrong reports as absent. Declaring `size_of_it :: (value: Z) -> s32`
        \\against a requirement returning `s64`, or adding a parameter the
        \\requirement does not have, both produce
        \\`error: instance 'Z :: K' does not satisfy #minimal(size_of_it)` rather than
        \\a separate mismatch message. When a formula fails, comparing the supplied
        \\signature against the class declaration character by character is the way
        \\to read it. The message re-renders the formula from its parse, so grouping
        \\parentheses are dropped: `#minimal((head_of, tail_of) | whole_of)` reports
        \\as `#minimal(head_of, tail_of | whole_of)`.
    );
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\A supplied method that matches no requirement at all is reported
        \\separately, once the minimal formula is satisfied:
        \\`error: instance method hint does not match any declaration in typeclass 'K'`.
        \\An instance may therefore not carry a helper of its own; helpers belong
        \\outside the instance body.
    );
    note(34, #string,oneline
        \\A `#modify` block on an instance method changes the signature key the
        \\compiler computes, so the instance fails its `#minimal` check even when the
        \\body is correct. Express the condition as a `where` clause on the instance
        \\instead; that path is checked by evaluating the clause, not by comparing
        \\keys.
    );
    table_header(35, "Diagnostic   —   Cause");
    table_row(36, "`does not satisfy #minimal(...)`   |   the formula is unsatisfied, or a member's signature differs");
    table_row(37, "`does not satisfy the implicit minimal definition; missing ...`   |   no directive, and a defaultless requirement was not supplied");
    table_row(38, "`instance method X does not match any declaration in typeclass 'C'`   |   a supplied member matches no requirement");
    table_row(39, "`#minimal references unknown typeclass method nope`   |   an atom names something the class does not declare");
    table_row(40, "`duplicate #minimal directive on typeclass 'K'`   |   two directives on one class");
}

render_section_70 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\An instance supplies a class's members for particular type arguments. It is
        \\written `<head> :: instance Class { ... }`, where the head names the type or
        \\types the instance is for and the body holds the methods and associated
        \\type values. An instance has no name of its own and is never referred to
        \\directly; it is found by the head, through the ordinary overload machinery
        \\described in How an Instance Is Chosen.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Area_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(area_of) {
        \\    area_of :: (value: T) -> f64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Rect :: struct { w: f64; h: f64; }
        \\Disc :: struct { r: $T; }
        \\
        \\Rect :: instance Area_Of {
        \\    area_of :: (value: Rect) -> f64 { return value.w * value.h; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Disc($T) :: instance Area_Of where Is_Float(T) {
        \\    area_of :: (value: Disc(T)) -> f64 { return 3.14159 * cast(f64)(value.r * value.r); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    r: Rect = .{ .w = 2.0, .h = 3.0 };
        \\    d: Disc(f32) = .{ .r = 1.0 };
        \\    print("% %\n", area_of(r), area_of(d));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\6 3.14159
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The head is every token before the `::`. The parser collects those tokens
        \\raw, then re-lexes and re-parses them as a type annotation, which is why a
        \\head may be any type expression and why a malformed one reports the text it
        \\collected: `Z + :: instance K` is `error: invalid instance head 'Z+'`. The
        \\`::` is what ends the scan, so omitting it does not give a bad head; it
        \\gives no instance at all, and the declaration fails as
        \\`error: unexpected token 'instance'`.
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\The head supplies exactly one argument per class parameter. For a
        \\one-parameter class the whole head is that argument; for a class with more
        \\than one, the head is a parenthesised tuple read left to right. A head with
        \\the wrong number of arguments cannot be substituted into the requirement
        \\signatures, so it surfaces as a `#minimal` failure rather than as an arity
        \\message.
    );
    subheading(5, "Head shapes");
    table_header(6, "Head   —   Matches");
    table_row(7, "`s64`   |   one nominal type");
    table_row(8, "`Box($A)`   |   every instantiation of the generic struct `Box`");
    table_row(9, "`Pair(s64, $B)`   |   a partially concrete instantiation");
    table_row(10, "`Box`   |   the bare constructor, for a class with a `(type) -> type` parameter");
    table_row(11, "`$T`   |   any type; narrowed by `where`");
    table_row(12, "`[]$T`   |   a view over any element type");
    table_row(13, "`[..]$T`   |   a dynamic array");
    table_row(14, "`*$T`   |   a pointer");
    table_row(15, "`[*]const $T`   |   a const many-pointer");
    table_row(16, "`?$T`   |   the built-in option type");
    table_row(17, "`$E!$T`   |   the built-in result type, error first");
    table_row(18, "`(A, B)`   |   one argument per parameter of a multi-parameter class");
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Label_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(label_of) {
        \\    label_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Box  :: struct { value: $A; }
        \\Pair :: struct { a: $A; b: $B; }
        \\
        \\s64          :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: s64) -> string { return "s64"; } }
        \\Box($A)      :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: Box(A)) -> string { return "Box"; } }
        \\$T           :: instance Label_Of where Is_Float(T) { label_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "float"; } }
        \\[]$T         :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: []T) -> string { return "view"; } }
        \\*$T          :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: *T) -> string { return "pointer"; } }
        \\[*]const $T  :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: [*]const T) -> string { return "const many-pointer"; } }
        \\?$T          :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: ?T) -> string { return "option"; } }
        \\$E!$T        :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: E!T) -> string { return "result"; } }
        \\Pair(s64,$B) :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: Pair(s64, B)) -> string { return "Pair(s64, B)"; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: s64 = 1;
        \\    b: Box(string) = .{ .value = "x" };
        \\    f: f64 = 1.5;
        \\    storage: [3]s64;
        \\    v: []s64 = storage[..];
        \\    p: *s64 = *n;
        \\    m: [*]const s64 = v.data;
        \\    o: ?s64 = .Some(2);
        \\    r: string!s64 = .Ok(3);
        \\    q: Pair(s64, string) = .{ .a = 1, .b = "y" };
        \\    print("% % % % % % % % %\n",
        \\        label_of(n), label_of(b), label_of(f), label_of(v), label_of(p),
        \\        label_of(m), label_of(o), label_of(r), label_of(q));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\s64 Box float view pointer const many-pointer option result Pair(s64, B)
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\A `$X` anywhere in the head declares a section generic that is bound when
        \\the head is matched. Inside the body it is spelled without the `$`, as
        \\`Box(A)` and `[]T` above, because the binder is already introduced. The
        \\body may use it in parameter annotations, in return types, and in
        \\associated type values.
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Structural heads match on the exact form, not on what converts to it. The
        \\`[]$T` instance does not accept a `[..]s64` or a `[*]s64`, and the
        \\`[*]const $T` instance does not accept a plain `[*]s64`; each is
        \\`error: no overload 'label_of' accepts these arguments`. Where two forms
        \\should behave alike, declare both heads, as the program above declares
        \\`[]$T` and `[*]const $T` separately.
    );
    subheading(23, "Tuple heads");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\A class with several parameters is keyed on the whole argument tuple, so
        \\`(s64, string)` and `(string, s64)` are different instances. This is what
        \\makes asymmetric operations expressible: a program may give `Mul` a
        \\`(Vector, f64)` instance and an `(f64, Vector)` instance with different
        \\bodies. A tuple head may mix concrete types, section generics, and a
        \\repeated binder.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Convert :: typeclass (From: type, To: type) #minimal(convert) {
        \\    convert :: (value: From, #empty: To) -> To;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Route :: typeclass (A: type, B: type, C: type) #minimal(route) {
        \\    route :: (a: A, b: B, c: C) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Flag :: struct { on: bool; }
        \\
        \\(s64, string) :: instance Convert {
        \\    convert :: (value: s64, #empty: string) -> string { return "n" + show(value); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\($T, T) :: instance Convert where Is_Float(T) {
        \\    convert :: (value: T, #empty: T) -> T { return value + value; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\(Flag, $T) :: instance Convert where Is_Integer(T) {
        \\    convert :: (value: Flag, #empty: T) -> T { if value.on return cast(T)1; return cast(T)0; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\(s64, string, Flag) :: instance Route {
        \\    route :: (a: s64, b: string, c: Flag) -> string { return show(a) + b + show(c.on); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", convert(7, string));
        \\    print("%\n", convert(1.5, f64));
        \\    f: Flag = .{ .on = true };
        \\    print("%\n", convert(f, s64));
        \\    print("%\n", route(2, "-", f));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\n7
        \\3
        \\1
        \\2-1
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\`($T, T)` is a single binder used twice: it matches only when both
        \\arguments are the same type. The `#empty:` parameter carries the second
        \\class parameter into the signature so that a call can decide it; witness
        \\parameters are covered in Compile-Time and Type-Only Parameters. Giving a
        \\two-parameter class a bare head such as `s64 :: instance Convert` is
        \\`error: instance 's64 :: Convert' does not satisfy #minimal(convert)`,
        \\because nothing binds `To`.
    );
    subheading(28, "Instance-level `where`");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\A `where` clause after the class name guards the whole instance. It is what
        \\turns a blanket head into a targeted one, and it is evaluated at compile
        \\time against the bound head arguments.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Sized_Note :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(note_of) {
        \\    note_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Sized_Note where Is_Integer(T) && size_of(T) <= 4 {
        \\    note_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "small integer"; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Sized_Note where Is_Integer(T) where size_of(T) > 4 {
        \\    note_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "wide integer"; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Sized_Note where Is_Float(T) {
        \\    note_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "float"; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: s16 = 1;
        \\    b: s64 = 1;
        \\    c: f32 = 1.0;
        \\    print("% / % / %\n", note_of(a), note_of(b), note_of(c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\small integer / wide integer / float
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Several `where` clauses may be chained and are conjoined, and a single
        \\clause may join conditions with `&&`; the two spellings above are
        \\equivalent. A clause is an ordinary compile-time expression, so a type
        \\predicate, `size_of`, an equality on types, and an evidence query all
        \\work. The clauses are copied onto every member of the instance, so an
        \\associated type value is guarded exactly as the methods are: three blanket
        \\instances can supply three different associated types for three disjoint
        \\families of type.
    );
    note(33, #string,oneline
        \\A `#modify` block is not accepted on an instance head:
        \\`$T :: instance C #modify { ... }` is `error: unexpected token 'modify'`.
        \\On an instance method it parses but breaks the `#minimal` check. Instance
        \\conditions belong in `where`.
    );
    subheading(34, "Instance bodies");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\A body holds two kinds of member, matching the class: a method, written
        \\like any procedure but with the head's type arguments substituted in, and
        \\an associated type value, written `Name :: <type>;`. A body may be empty,
        \\which is what a marker class and a negative instance use. A method may end
        \\at a `#magic` intrinsic instead of a body; the Prelude's arithmetic
        \\instances are written that way, as
        \\`'+' :: (left: T, right: T) -> Output #magic "int_add";`.
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\Every member must correspond to a declaration in the class. A member the
        \\class does not declare is `error: function 'g' has no owning typeclass`,
        \\which is also what naming an unknown class reports when the body holds a
        \\method; with an empty body the same mistake is
        \\`error: unknown typeclass 'Nonexistent'`. Associated type names are checked
        \\too: `error: typeclass 'Registry' has no associated type 'Wrong'`.
    );
    bullet(37, #string,oneline
        \\There is no orphan rule. An instance may pair anyone's type with anyone's
        \\class, including a standard-library type with a standard-library class.
        \\Which instance wins is decided by specificity, not by locality; see
        \\Evidence, Constraints, and Negative Instances.
    );
    bullet(38, #string,oneline
        \\`<head> :: instance !Class { }` declares that the head explicitly does not
        \\implement the class. Negative instances are covered in Evidence,
        \\Constraints, and Negative Instances.
    );
}

render_section_71 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\An associated type is a type that the instance chooses rather than the
        \\caller. The class declares the name and the instance supplies a value for
        \\it, so a single class parameter can carry a whole family of dependent
        \\types: `Indexable` names the element an index produces, `Try` names the
        \\output and the residual of a fallible carrier, `Add` names the type a sum
        \\has.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Collection :: typeclass (C: type) #minimal(first_of, count_of) {
        \\    Elem :: type;
        \\    first_of :: (container: C) -> Elem;
        \\    count_of :: (container: C) -> s64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Words  :: struct { items: []string; }
        \\Scores :: struct { items: []s64; }
        \\
        \\Words :: instance Collection {
        \\    Elem :: string;
        \\    first_of :: (container: Words) -> string { return container.items[0]; }
        \\    count_of :: (container: Words) -> s64 { return container.items.count; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Scores :: instance Collection {
        \\    Elem :: s64;
        \\    first_of :: (container: Scores) -> s64 { return container.items[0]; }
        \\    count_of :: (container: Scores) -> s64 { return container.items.count; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    w_storage: [2]string;
        \\    w_storage[0] = "alpha";
        \\    w_storage[1] = "beta";
        \\    s_storage: [2]s64;
        \\    s_storage[0] = 4;
        \\    s_storage[1] = 5;
        \\
        \\    w: Words  = .{ .items = w_storage[..] };
        \\    s: Scores = .{ .items = s_storage[..] };
        \\
        \\    a: Collection(Words).Elem  = first_of(w);
        \\    b: Collection(Scores).Elem = first_of(s);
        \\    print("% %\n", a, b);
        \\    print("% %\n", count_of(w), count_of(s));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\alpha 4
        \\2 2
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\On the class side the member is `Elem :: type;`: a name followed by a type
        \\expression and a semicolon, with no parentheses, which is what tells the
        \\parser it is not a method. On the instance side the same name is bound to a
        \\concrete type, `Elem :: string;`. Inside the class, the name is used as an
        \\ordinary type in the requirement signatures, and the minimal check
        \\substitutes the instance's value before comparing signatures, which is why
        \\`first_of :: (container: Words) -> string` satisfies
        \\`first_of :: (container: C) -> Elem`.
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\There is no default value. The type expression the class writes is a
        \\requirement, not a fallback: writing a concrete type such as `Key :: s64;`
        \\on the class side and omitting `Key` from the instance still fails the
        \\minimal check, because the requirement signatures keep an unresolved `Key`
        \\in them and nothing the instance supplies can match. The class side is not
        \\a constraint on the choice either: an instance that answers `Key :: string;`
        \\to a class that wrote `Key :: s64;` is accepted. The conventional spelling
        \\is therefore `Name :: type;`.
    );
    subheading(5, "Projection");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\An associated type is read back with `Class(args).Name`. This is a type
        \\annotation form: it names a type and may appear anywhere a type may. The
        \\arguments are the class's parameters, so the projection is resolved by
        \\finding the instance for those arguments and reading its value.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Widen :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(widen) {
        \\    Wide :: type;
        \\    widen :: (value: T) -> Wide;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Widen where Is_Integer(T) { Wide :: s64; widen :: (value: T) -> s64 { return cast(s64)value; } }
        \\$T :: instance Widen where Is_Float(T)   { Wide :: f64; widen :: (value: T) -> f64 { return cast(f64)value; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: s16 = 7;
        \\    b: f32 = 1.5;
        \\    x: Widen(s16).Wide = widen(a);
        \\    y: Widen(f32).Wide = widen(b);
        \\    print("% %\n", x, y);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\7 1.5
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\`Widen(s16).Wide` is `s64` and `Widen(f32).Wide` is `f64`, decided by the
        \\same `where` guards that decide which `widen` runs. The instance-level
        \\clauses are copied onto the associated type value as well as onto the
        \\methods, so a projection can never resolve through an instance whose guard
        \\is false.
    );
    subheading(10, "Projection in generic code");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The arguments of a projection may be generic binders, which is the form
        \\that matters: a generic procedure states its return type in terms of the
        \\caller's type without knowing it.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Collection :: typeclass (C: type) #minimal(first_of) {
        \\    Elem :: type;
        \\    first_of :: (container: C) -> Elem;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Words  :: struct { items: []string; }
        \\Scores :: struct { items: []s64; }
        \\Words  :: instance Collection { Elem :: string; first_of :: (c: Words)  -> string { return c.items[0]; } }
        \\Scores :: instance Collection { Elem :: s64;    first_of :: (c: Scores) -> s64    { return c.items[0]; } }
        \\
        \\head_of :: (container: $C) -> Collection(C).Elem where Collection(C) {
        \\    return first_of(container);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    ws: [1]string; ws[0] = "alpha";
        \\    ss: [1]s64;    ss[0] = 9;
        \\    w: Words  = .{ .items = ws[..] };
        \\    s: Scores = .{ .items = ss[..] };
        \\    print("% %\n", head_of(w), head_of(s));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\alpha 9
    );
    note(14, #string,oneline
        \\A projection works in a return annotation but not in a parameter
        \\annotation. Adding `fallback: Collection(C).Elem` as a second parameter to
        \\`head_of` above and calling it makes the call
        \\`error: generic argument does not match call head_of`, even when the
        \\argument's type is exactly what the projection resolves to. Take such a
        \\value through a second generic binder and constrain it separately.
    );
    subheading(15, "The argument-free projection");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\`Class.Name` with no arguments is accepted when exactly one instance in the
        \\program supplies that associated type. It is a convenience for a class with
        \\a single implementation, and it stops being legal the moment a second
        \\instance appears.
    );
    code_block(17, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Registry :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(key_of) {
        \\    Key :: type;
        \\    key_of :: (value: T) -> Key;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Session :: struct { id: s64; }
        \\Session :: instance Registry {
        \\    Key :: s64;
        \\    key_of :: (value: Session) -> s64 { return value.id; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Session = .{ .id = 12 };
        \\    k: Registry.Key = key_of(s);
        \\    print("%\n", k);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\12
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Adding a second instance turns the same annotation into an error, and the
        \\fix is to write the arguments.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\Registry :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(key_of) {
        \\    Key :: type;
        \\    key_of :: (value: T) -> Key;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Session :: struct { id: s64; }
        \\Ticket  :: struct { code: string; }
        \\
        \\Session :: instance Registry { Key :: s64;    key_of :: (value: Session) -> s64    { return value.id; } }
        \\Ticket  :: instance Registry { Key :: string; key_of :: (value: Ticket)  -> string { return value.code; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    k: Registry.Key = 1;
        \\    _ := k;
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\error: associated type 'Registry.Key' is ambiguous; provide type arguments
        \\   --> assoc.glo:13:8
        \\   |
        \\13 |     k: Registry.Key = 1;
        \\   |        ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    subheading(22, "Why the projection is not an expression");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\`Class(args).name` is also the spelling for a scoped typeclass method
        \\reference, and that is the reading the expression parser takes. The two
        \\forms are told apart by position, not by the name: in a type annotation the
        \\member is looked up among the class's associated types, and in an
        \\expression it is looked up among the class's methods.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Registry :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(key_of) {
        \\    Key :: type;
        \\    key_of :: (value: T) -> Key;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Session :: struct { id: s64; }
        \\Session :: instance Registry {
        \\    Key :: s64;
        \\    key_of :: (value: Session) -> s64 { return value.id; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    s: Session = .{ .id = 12 };
        \\    k: Registry(Session).Key = Registry(Session).key_of(s);
        \\    print("%\n", k);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(25, "text", #string
        \\12
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The annotation projects the associated type and the initializer calls the
        \\method, from the same spelling of the same class at the same argument.
        \\Using an associated type in expression position therefore reports a missing
        \\method: `t := Registry(Session).Key;` is
        \\`error: typeclass 'Registry' has no method 'Key'`. Where the projected type
        \\is wanted as a value, declare a binding with the projection as its
        \\annotation and take `type_of` of the binding.
    );
    subheading(27, "Projecting another class");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\A projection may appear inside an instance, including in the value of that
        \\instance's own associated type. This is how a derived type is threaded
        \\through: the instance defers to whatever `Add` decided rather than
        \\committing to a concrete type.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Doubler :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(twice) {
        \\    Result_Of :: type;
        \\    twice :: (value: T) -> Result_Of;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Doubler where Add(T, T) {
        \\    Result_Of :: Add(T, T).Output;
        \\    twice :: (value: T) -> Add(T, T).Output { return value + value; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: s64 = 21;
        \\    x: f64 = 1.25;
        \\    print("% %\n", twice(n), twice(x));
        \\    d: Doubler(s64).Result_Of = twice(n);
        \\    print("%\n", d);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\42 2.5
        \\42
    );
    table_header(31, "Diagnostic   —   Cause");
    table_row(32, "`no associated type value for Registry.Key`   |   the matching instance does not supply it");
    table_row(33, "`associated type 'Registry.Key' is ambiguous; provide type arguments`   |   the argument-free form with more than one instance");
    table_row(34, "`associated type arguments must follow the typeclass name`   |   written `Registry.Key(A)` instead of `Registry(A).Key`");
    table_row(35, "`typeclass 'Registry' has no associated type 'Wrong'`   |   an instance supplies a name the class does not declare");
    table_row(36, "`cyclic associated type definition involving 'Cy(A).Elem'`   |   a value that projects itself");
}

render_section_72 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Applying a class to a type produces a compile-time boolean: `Marker(s64)`
        \\asks whether `s64` has a `Marker` instance and folds to `1` or `0` during
        \\checking. This is the same query the compiler runs when it selects an
        \\instance, exposed to the program so that generic code can branch on it or
        \\demand it.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Marker :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal() {}
        \\Other  :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal() {}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Marker where Is_Integer(T) {}
        \\$T :: instance Other  where Is_Integer(T) {}
        \\
        \\MARKED_S64 :: Marker(s64);
        \\MARKED_F64 :: Marker(f64);
        \\
        \\report :: (value: $T) -> string {
        \\    #if Marker(T) return "marked";
        \\    else return "unmarked";
        \\}
        \\
        \\both :: (value: $T) -> string where Marker(T) && Other(T) { return "both"; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    #assert Marker(s16);
        \\    n: s64 = 1;
        \\    f: f64 = 1.0;
        \\    print("constants = % %\n", MARKED_S64, MARKED_F64);
        \\    print("static if = % %\n", report(n), report(f));
        \\    print("where     = %\n", both(n));
        \\    print("comptime  = %\n", #comptime Marker(u8));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\constants = 1 0
        \\static if = marked unmarked
        \\where     = both
        \\comptime  = 1
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The query folds only in the contexts the constraint evaluator understands.
        \\A `where` clause accepts a bare query or several joined with `&&`; every
        \\other shape of `where` expression containing a query is rejected, because
        \\the evaluator recognises the call form itself rather than evaluating an
        \\arbitrary expression that contains it.
    );
    table_header(4, "Context   —   Folds");
    table_row(5, "`where Class(T)`   |   yes");
    table_row(6, "`where A(T) && B(T)`   |   yes");
    table_row(7, "`#if Class(T)`, `#if !Class(T)`   |   yes");
    table_row(8, "`EV :: Class(T);` at file scope   |   yes");
    table_row(9, "`#comptime Class(T)`, `#assert Class(T)`   |   yes");
    table_row(10, "`where !Class(T)`   |   no: `error: non-constant function call`");
    table_row(11, "`where Class(T) == false` or `where A(T) || B(T)`   |   no: `error: non-constant function call`");
    table_row(12, "a runtime expression   |   no: `error: internal error: 'Marker' is called but was never lowered`");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\A negated or disjoint constraint is therefore written with `#if` inside the
        \\body rather than with `where` on the signature, or split into two
        \\procedures. Wrapping a query in a helper predicate does not work either:
        \\the evaluator needs to see the class name at the call site, so
        \\`where P(T)` where `P` returns `Class(T)` is
        \\`error: constraint failed for use_it`.
    );
    note(14, #string,oneline
        \\The expression form takes exactly one type argument, whatever the class's
        \\arity. A multi-parameter class queried this way answers `0` rather than
        \\reporting anything, so `PartialEq(s64)` is `0` even though `s64` compares
        \\with `==`; supplying both arguments is
        \\`error: PartialEq(T) expects one type argument`. Query a multi-parameter
        \\class inside a `where` clause, where the full argument list is accepted.
    );
    code_block(15, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\EQ_S64        :: Eq(s64);
        \\EQ_F64        :: Eq(f64);
        \\PARTIAL_EQ_S64 :: PartialEq(s64);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("Eq(s64)=% Eq(f64)=% PartialEq(s64)=%\n", EQ_S64, EQ_F64, PARTIAL_EQ_S64);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(16, "text", #string
        \\Eq(s64)=1 Eq(f64)=0 PartialEq(s64)=0
    );
    subheading(17, "Negative instances");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\`<head> :: instance !Class { }` declares that the head does not implement
        \\the class. It is not the absence of an instance but a statement of absence,
        \\and it overrides any positive instance that would otherwise match: evidence
        \\is positive and not negative.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Tagged :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(tag_of) {
        \\    tag_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Tagged where Is_Integer(T) { tag_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "integer"; } }
        \\u8 :: instance !Tagged {}
        \\
        \\TAGGED_S64 :: Tagged(s64);
        \\TAGGED_U8  :: Tagged(u8);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: s64 = 1;
        \\    print("Tagged(s64)=% Tagged(u8)=%\n", TAGGED_S64, TAGGED_U8);
        \\    print("%\n", tag_of(n));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\Tagged(s64)=1 Tagged(u8)=0
        \\integer
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\The exclusion reaches dispatch as well as queries. With the declaration
        \\above, calling `tag_of` on a `u8` value is
        \\`error: no overload 'tag_of' accepts these arguments`: the blanket instance
        \\matches the argument, then loses its evidence and is dropped. A generic
        \\procedure guarded by `where Tagged(T)` reports the constraint instead, as
        \\`error: constraint failed for guarded`.
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\A negative instance's body is not checked and is never used, so it may be
        \\empty even for a class with required methods, and a body written into one
        \\is dead. Its purpose is to carve an exception out of a blanket instance
        \\that is otherwise right. The Prelude uses it for floating point:
        \\`$T :: instance !Eq where Is_Float(T) {}` keeps floats comparable with `==`
        \\while denying them the total-equality marker, because NaN is not equal to
        \\itself.
    );
    subheading(23, "Coherence and overlap");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Glosso has no orphan rule and no coherence check at declaration time. Two
        \\instances may cover the same type, and the program compiles until a call
        \\needs one of them. Overlap is reported at the use site, with a note on each
        \\of the two instances that tied.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Label_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(label_of) {
        \\    label_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Label_Of where Is_Integer(T) { label_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "one"; } }
        \\$T :: instance Label_Of where Is_Integer(T) { label_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "two"; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    n: s64 = 1;
        \\    print("%\n", label_of(n));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\error: ambiguous typeclass operation 'label_of' for these argument types
        \\   --> overlap.glo:12:18
        \\   |
        \\12 |     print("%\n", label_of(n));
        \\   |                  ^^^^^^^^
        \\note: first matching instance is declared here
        \\  --> overlap.glo:8:47
        \\  |
        \\8 | $T :: instance Label_Of where Is_Integer(T) { label_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "two"; } }
        \\  |                                               ^^^^^^^^
        \\note: another matching instance is declared here
        \\  --> overlap.glo:7:47
        \\  |
        \\7 | $T :: instance Label_Of where Is_Integer(T) { label_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "one"; } }
        \\  |                                               ^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\Most overlap is not ambiguous, because selection scores candidates and a
        \\concrete parameter outranks a generic one. A specific instance placed
        \\beside a blanket instance wins for the type it names and leaves the rest to
        \\the blanket, with no annotation and no ordering rule.
    );
    code_block(28, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Label_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(label_of) {
        \\    label_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T  :: instance Label_Of where Is_Integer(T) { label_of :: (value: T)   -> string { return "an integer"; } }
        \\s64 :: instance Label_Of                     { label_of :: (value: s64) -> string { return "specifically s64"; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: s64 = 1;
        \\    b: s16 = 2;
        \\    print("% / %\n", label_of(a), label_of(b));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(29, "text", #string
        \\specifically s64 / an integer
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\The same freedom applies across module boundaries. A program may add an
        \\instance for a standard-library type and a standard-library class, and it
        \\outranks the Prelude's blanket instance for the same type by being
        \\concrete.
    );
    code_block(31, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\u8 :: instance Show { show :: (value: u8) -> string { return "byte(" + show(cast(s64)value) + ")"; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b: u8 = 7;
        \\    n: s64 = 7;
        \\    print("% %\n", show(b), show(n));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(32, "text", #string
        \\byte(7) 7
    );
    note(33, #string,oneline
        \\Evidence and dispatch are different questions. A method call is ordinary
        \\overload resolution, so it goes through implicit coercions and a concrete
        \\instance can capture an argument of a different but convertible type, even
        \\though the evidence query for that type is false. Where an instance must
        \\key on one type exactly, use a blanket head with `where T == s64` rather
        \\than a concrete head.
    );
    code_block(34, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Label_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(label_of) {
        \\    label_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\s64 :: instance Label_Of { label_of :: (value: s64) -> string { return "the s64 instance"; } }
        \\
        \\LABEL_U32 :: Label_Of(u32);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: u32 = 3;
        \\    print("call    = %\n", label_of(c));
        \\    print("evidence = %\n", LABEL_U32);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(35, "text", #string
        \\call    = the s64 instance
        \\evidence = 0
    );
}

render_section_73 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Glosso has no separate constraint-solving pass for typeclasses. A call to
        \\a class method is resolved by the ordinary overload machinery, over the
        \\set of every instance method and every class default that carries that
        \\name. Instance selection is overload resolution, and every rule that
        \\governs overloads governs it.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\That single design decision produces most of the behaviour worth
        \\memorising. A more specific instance outranks a more general one. An
        \\implicit conversion applied to an argument can change which instance is
        \\selected. Two equally good instances are a diagnostic at the call site,
        \\not at the declaration.
    );
    subheading(2, "The resolution steps");
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The checker performs these steps in order for every call whose name is
        \\owned by a typeclass. Steps 5 and 6 exist only to break ties, and a
        \\program that never writes overlapping instances never reaches them.
    );
    table_header(4, "Step   —   What the checker does");
    table_row(5, "1   |   Collect every declaration with that name. A call written `Class(T).m(...)` drops candidates that do not belong to `Class`.");
    table_row(6, "2   |   Unify and coerce every argument against each candidate's parameter annotations, accumulating a specificity score.");
    table_row(7, "3   |   Run the candidate's `#modify` block, then its `where` clauses, then check that the instance head itself has evidence.");
    table_row(8, "4   |   If any surviving match is a real instance method, discard every class-default match.");
    table_row(9, "5   |   Sort by implicit `#empty` witness first, then by score, highest first.");
    table_row(10, "6   |   If the top two still tie, narrow by the expected return type, then by compile-time-number defaulting.");
    table_row(11, "7   |   A remaining tie is `error: ambiguous typeclass operation '<name>' for these argument types`.");
    subheading(12, "The specificity score");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\The score is a small integer accumulated while the arguments are matched.
        \\It measures how much of the candidate's signature was pinned down by the
        \\call rather than left generic, so a signature that names a concrete type
        \\outscores one that names a type variable.
    );
    table_header(14, "Contribution   —   When it applies");
    table_row(15, "`+2`   |   A parameter with a concrete annotation that the argument coerced to.");
    table_row(16, "`+1`   |   A generic parameter, when the candidate is an instance method or a class default.");
    table_row(17, "`+2`   |   An argument that is a typeclass method reference resolved against a concrete function parameter.");
    table_row(18, "`+10_000`   |   Every argument's own type equalled its parameter type, with no coercion anywhere.");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\The exactness bonus dominates everything else, which is why an instance
        \\that matches the argument type on the nose beats one that would need a
        \\widening conversion, however concrete the second one is. The primary sort
        \\key is not the score at all: a candidate that consumed an implicit
        \\`#empty:` type witness sorts behind one that did not, and only then does
        \\the score decide.
    );
    subheading(20, "Specific instances beat general ones");
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\A blanket instance guarded by a `where` predicate and a concrete instance
        \\for one type may both apply. When both match exactly, the concrete one
        \\scores `+2` against the blanket's `+1` and wins.
    );
    code_block(22, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Label_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(label_of) {
        \\    label_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\    describe :: (value: T) -> string { return "a " + label_of(value); }
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T  :: instance Label_Of where Is_Integer(T) {
        \\    label_of :: (value: T) -> string { return "integer"; }
        \\}
        \\s64 :: instance Label_Of {
        \\    label_of :: (value: s64) -> string { return "s64"; }
        \\    describe :: (value: s64) -> string { return "exactly s64"; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: s64 = 1;
        \\    b: u16 = 2;
        \\    print("%\n", label_of(a));
        \\    print("%\n", label_of(b));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(a));
        \\    print("%\n", describe(b));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\s64
        \\integer
        \\exactly s64
        \\exactly s64
    );
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`label_of(a)` scores `10002` for the `s64` instance against `10001` for
        \\the blanket, so the concrete instance is chosen. `label_of(b)` reverses
        \\it: `u16` matches the blanket's `T` exactly and earns the exactness bonus,
        \\while the `s64` instance needs a widening conversion and scores only `2`.
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The last line is the one that surprises. `describe(b)` has two candidates:
        \\the class default, which matches `u16` exactly for `10001`, and the `s64`
        \\instance method, which scores `2`. Step 4 runs before the sort, so the
        \\default is discarded the moment any instance matched, and the low-scoring
        \\instance method is what remains. An instance method always beats a class
        \\default, regardless of score, because the default exists to be overridden.
    );
    note(26, #string,oneline
        \\Because selection runs through the ordinary implicit conversions, an
        \\instance written for one concrete type can capture arguments of every
        \\type that converts to it. With no blanket instance in the program, there
        \\is nothing to score higher and the conversion goes unremarked.
    );
    code_block(27, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Tag_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(tag_of) {
        \\    tag_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\s64 :: instance Tag_Of { tag_of :: (value: s64) -> string { return "s64 instance"; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    u: u16 = 1;
        \\    print("u16 -> %\n", tag_of(u));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(28, "text", #string
        \\u16 -> s64 instance
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\An instance keyed on exactly one type is written as a blanket instance
        \\whose predicate is a type equality, because a `where` clause is checked
        \\after the argument has been bound and admits no conversion.
        \\`$T :: instance Tag_Of where T == s64` matches `s64` and nothing else, and
        \\`where Is_Integer(T) && T != s64` is the complementary instance.
    );
    subheading(30, "Dispatch by the expected return type");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\A method whose class parameter appears only in return position gives the
        \\arguments nothing to decide. Every instance then matches equally well, the
        \\scores tie, and step 6 consults the type the call site expects.
    );
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Unit_Of :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(unit_of) {
        \\    unit_of :: () -> T;
        \\}
        \\
        \\s64 :: instance Unit_Of { unit_of :: () -> s64 { return 7; } }
        \\f64 :: instance Unit_Of { unit_of :: () -> f64 { return 1.5; } }
        \\
        \\pick :: () -> f64 { return unit_of(); }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: s64 = unit_of();
        \\    print("annotated = %\n", a);
        \\    print("returned  = %\n", pick());
        \\    b: f64 = 0.0;
        \\    b = unit_of();
        \\    print("assigned  = %\n", b);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\annotated = 7
        \\returned  = 1.5
        \\assigned  = 1.5
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\An annotated binding, a `return` in a function with a declared return
        \\type, and an assignment to an already-typed variable all supply an
        \\expected type. Nothing else does. `x := unit_of();` and
        \\`cast(f64)unit_of()` both leave the call undirected and are
        \\`error: ambiguous typeclass operation 'unit_of' for these argument types`,
        \\with a note pointing at each of the two instances.
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\The narrowing prefers a candidate whose return type equals the expected
        \\type exactly; if exactly one candidate's return type merely converts to
        \\it, that one is taken instead, and two exact matches leave the tie
        \\standing. Compile-time-number defaulting is the second tie-breaker: an
        \\undecided numeric literal matches every numeric instance with a conversion
        \\and never exactly, so it would otherwise read as ambiguous. Nothing
        \\decided the literal, so its default type dispatches.
    );
    code_block(36, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Twice :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(twice) { twice :: (v: T) -> T; }
        \\
        \\s64 :: instance Twice { twice :: (v: s64) -> s64 { return v * 2; } }
        \\f64 :: instance Twice { twice :: (v: f64) -> f64 { return v * 2.0; } }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("twice(3)   = %\n", twice(3));
        \\    print("twice(2.5) = %\n", twice(2.5));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(37, "text", #string
        \\twice(3)   = 6
        \\twice(2.5) = 5
    );
    subheading(38, "Coherence, orphans, and evidence");
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\Two identical instances are legal to declare. Coherence is checked lazily,
        \\at the call that cannot choose between them, and the diagnostic names both
        \\declarations: `error: ambiguous typeclass operation 'tag_of' for these
        \\argument types`, then
        \\`note: first matching instance is declared here` and
        \\`note: another matching instance is declared here`. The instance declared
        \\last is reported first, because candidates are matched in reverse
        \\declaration order.
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\There is no orphan rule. A program may declare an instance of a standard
        \\class for a standard type, and it wins wherever it is more specific:
        \\`u8 :: instance Show` outranks the Prelude's
        \\`$T :: instance Show where Is_Integer(T)` for `u8` values and leaves every
        \\other integer alone. Specificity, not locality, decides.
    );
    paragraph(41, #string,oneline
        \\Step 3 is where a class-level `where` takes effect. The clause is a
        \\superclass constraint evaluated when evidence is asked for, not when the
        \\instance is declared, so an instance of `Refined :: typeclass (T: type)
        \\where Base(T)` for a type with no `Base` instance compiles and is then
        \\dropped from every candidate list. The call reports
        \\`error: no overload 'sub_of' accepts these arguments` and lists the
        \\candidates it rejected.
    );
    bullet(42, #string,oneline
        \\Selection never consults a dictionary, so an instance method that is
        \\selected is called directly and instance dispatch costs nothing at run
        \\time. A class default and an instance method are distinguished only by
        \\which body was written where; both are ordinary declarations in the same
        \\candidate set.
    );
    note(43, #string,oneline
        \\A class default whose signature omits one of the class parameters can
        \\never be dispatched. For `Convert :: typeclass (From: type, To: type)`, a
        \\default `describe :: (value: From) -> string` leaves `To` undetermined and
        \\calling it is `error: no overload 'describe' accepts these arguments`.
        \\Give every class parameter a position in the signature; an anonymous
        \\`#empty:` witness parameter is the usual way to place one that carries no
        \\value.
    );
}

render_section_74 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A typeclass parameter carries a kind, written as an ordinary type
        \\annotation. `T: type` declares a parameter that stands for a type;
        \\`F: (type) -> type` declares one that stands for a type constructor, a
        \\generic type that has not yet been applied to its argument. A class over
        \\such a parameter is higher-kinded.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\Inside the class body the parameter is applied like a generic type:
        \\`F($A)` is the constructor at some inferred element type, and `F(B)` is
        \\the same constructor at another. This is what lets one method signature
        \\describe a transformation that changes the element type but keeps the
        \\container.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Container :: typeclass (F: (type) -> type) #minimal(mapc, unwrap) {
        \\    mapc   :: (fa: F($A), f: (A) -> $B) -> F(B);
        \\    unwrap :: (fa: F($A)) -> A;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Box  :: struct { value: $A; }
        \\Pair :: struct { first: $A; second: A; }
        \\
        \\Box :: instance Container {
        \\    mapc   :: (fa: Box($A), f: (A) -> $B) -> Box(B) { return .{ .value = f(fa.value) }; }
        \\    unwrap :: (fa: Box($A)) -> A { return fa.value; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Pair :: instance Container {
        \\    mapc   :: (fa: Pair($A), f: (A) -> $B) -> Pair(B) {
        \\        return .{ .first = f(fa.first), .second = f(fa.second) };
        \\    }
        \\    unwrap :: (fa: Pair($A)) -> A { return fa.first; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\double :: (x: s64) -> s64 { return x * 2; }
        \\tag    :: (x: s64) -> string { return "n"; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b: Box(s64)  = .{ .value = 21 };
        \\    p: Pair(s64) = .{ .first = 3, .second = 4 };
        \\    print("box  = %\n", unwrap(mapc(b, double)));
        \\    print("pair = %\n", unwrap(mapc(p, double)));
        \\    print("retype = %\n", unwrap(mapc(b, tag)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\box  = 42
        \\pair = 6
        \\retype = n
    );
    subheading(4, "Bare-constructor instance heads");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The instance head for a higher-kinded class is the bare constructor name.
        \\`Box :: instance Container` supplies `Box` itself, not `Box` at some
        \\element type, so a single instance covers every application of the
        \\constructor at once. Writing `Box($A) :: instance Container` would supply a
        \\type where a constructor is wanted.
    );
    table_header(6, "Class parameter   —   Instance head   —   Method parameter");
    table_row(7, "`T: type`   |   `Box($A) :: instance C`   |   `(value: Box(A))`");
    table_row(8, "`F: (type) -> type`   |   `Box :: instance C`   |   `(fa: Box($A))`");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\In both forms the method's own generics are introduced with `$` at their
        \\first occurrence and referred to without it afterwards. In the
        \\higher-kinded form the `$A` belongs to the method, not to the head, which
        \\is exactly why the head has no hole to fill.
    );
    subheading(10, "Evidence is asked at the applied type");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\Although the instance is registered against the bare constructor, an
        \\evidence query is answered for the applied type. `Box(s64)` and
        \\`Box(string)` both implement `Functor` on the strength of one
        \\`Box :: instance Functor`, and a type with no such instance is rejected
        \\with the element type in the message.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\
        \\Box   :: struct #derive(Functor) { value: $A; }
        \\Plain :: struct { n: s64; }
        \\
        \\count_functors :: (values: ...Functor) -> s64 { return values.count; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    b: Box(s64)    = .{ .value = 1 };
        \\    c: Box(string) = .{ .value = "x" };
        \\    print("functors = %\n", count_functors(b, c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\functors = 2
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\Passing a `Plain` to the same procedure is
        \\``error: variadic arg type `Plain` does not implement `Functor` in call
        \\`count_functors` ``. Typeclass-constrained variadics are covered in
        \\Typeclass-Constrained Variadics.
    );
    note(15, #string,oneline
        \\There is no source-level spelling for a higher-kinded evidence query. The
        \\constructor form `Container(Box)` is
        \\`error: generic struct Box needs type arguments`, and the applied form
        \\`Container(Box(s64))` is `error: non-constant function call`. Ask for the
        \\evidence through a constrained variadic or a `where` clause on a method
        \\that takes the applied type as an argument.
    );
    subheading(16, "Functor, Applicative, and Monad");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\The standard library declares three higher-kinded classes in
        \\`std/Typeclass`. They form a chain: each names the previous one as a
        \\superclass in its class-level `where`, so evidence for `Monad` requires
        \\evidence for `Applicative`, which requires `Functor`.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\Functor :: typeclass (F: (type) -> type) #derive(derive_functor) {
        \\    fmap :: (fa: F($A), f: (A) -> $B) -> F(B);
        \\}
        \\
        \\Applicative :: typeclass (F: (type) -> type) where Functor(F) #derive(derive_applicative) {
        \\    pure :: (x: $A) -> F(A);
        \\    ap   :: (ff: F(($A) -> $B), fa: F(A)) -> F(B);
        \\}
        \\
        \\Monad :: typeclass (M: (type) -> type) where Applicative(M) #derive(derive_monad) {
        \\    bind :: (ma: M($A), f: (A) -> M($B)) -> M(B);
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\All three carry a derive hook, so a one-field struct or a tagged union can
        \\request the instances instead of writing them. The derived `Applicative`
        \\and `Monad` for a struct handle one-field structs only; for a union they
        \\pick the single variant whose payload is the focused type parameter.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\
        \\Ident :: struct #derive(Functor, Applicative, Monad) { value: $A; }
        \\
        \\inc  :: (x: s64) -> s64 { return x + 1; }
        \\step :: (x: s64) -> Ident(s64) { return .{ .value = x * 10 }; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    i: Ident(s64) = .{ .value = 4 };
        \\    m := fmap(i, inc);
        \\    f: Ident((s64) -> s64) = .{ .value = inc };
        \\    a := ap(f, i);
        \\    b := bind(m, step);
        \\    print("fmap = %\n", m.value);
        \\    print("ap   = %\n", a.value);
        \\    print("bind = %\n", b.value);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\fmap = 5
        \\ap   = 5
        \\bind = 50
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`fmap`, `ap` and `bind` all dispatch on an argument whose type mentions
        \\the class parameter, so ordinary selection resolves them. Deriving
        \\Instances with `#derive` covers what the three macros generate, and
        \\Writing a Derive Macro covers how they generate it.
    );
    subheading(23, "`pure` cannot be called");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\`pure :: (x: $A) -> F(A)` mentions the class parameter only in return
        \\position. The expected-type narrowing described in How an Instance Is
        \\Chosen does not rescue it, because the candidate's return type is itself
        \\generic and never equals the expected type.
    );
    code_block(25, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\
        \\Ident :: struct #derive(Functor, Applicative) { value: $A; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Ident(s64) = pure(3);
        \\    print("pure = %\n", p.value);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(26, "text", #string
        \\error: no overload 'pure' accepts these arguments
        \\note: call pure cannot produce expected type Ident_int
        \\note: possible candidates for 'pure':
        \\candidate: pure :: (x: A) -> Ident(A)
        \\candidate: pure :: (x: A) -> F(A)
    );
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A hand-written instance does not help; the limitation is in the
        \\dispatcher, not in the derive macro. Construct the carrier directly
        \\instead: `p: Ident(s64) = .{ .value = 3 };` says the same thing and
        \\resolves without a class method.
    );
    subheading(28, "A worked example");
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\`examples/typeclasses.glo` in the compiler repository derives `Functor`
        \\for a generic tagged union and writes `Applicative` and `Monad` by hand on
        \\top of it. Both hand-written instance heads are the bare `Foo`.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\
        \\Foo :: union #derive(Functor) {
        \\    Some: $T;
        \\    None: void;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Foo :: instance Applicative {
        \\    pure :: (x: $A) -> Foo(A) {
        \\        return Foo.Some(x);
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    ap :: (ff: Foo(($A) -> $B), fa: Foo(A)) -> Foo(B) {
        \\        if ff == {
        \\            case .Some(function);
        \\                return fmap(fa, function);
        \\            case .None;
        \\                return .None;
        \\        }
        \\        return .None;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Foo :: instance Monad {
        \\    bind :: (ma: Foo($A), f: (A) -> Foo($B)) -> Foo(B) {
        \\        if ma == {
        \\            case .Some(input);
        \\                return f(input);
        \\            case .None;
        \\                return .None;
        \\        }
        \\        return .None;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\inc :: (x: s64) -> s64 { return x + 1; }
        \\to_option :: (x: s64) -> Foo(s64) { return Foo.Some(x + 10); }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    o := Foo.Some(42);
        \\    mapped := fmap(o, inc);
        \\    chained := bind(mapped, to_option);
        \\    if #pattern Foo.Some(total) = mapped && #pattern Foo.Some(chained_total) = chained
        \\        print("typeclass totals % %\n", total, chained_total);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\typeclass totals 43 53
    );
    bullet(32, #string,oneline
        \\A union payload is read through a pattern capture, never through
        \\`ma.Some`. Every derived instance emits `case Variant(payload);` arms for
        \\the same reason.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\A class-level `where` on a higher-kinded class behaves exactly as it does
        \\on a first-order one: it is evaluated when evidence is asked for, and an
        \\instance that fails it is silently unusable rather than rejected at its
        \\declaration.
    );
    bullet(34, #string,oneline
        \\A parameter of kind `(type) -> type` accepts a generic struct or a generic
        \\union. The built-in `?T` and `E!T` have no bare constructor spelling: the
        \\head `?$T :: instance C` supplies a type, and a lone `?` is
        \\`error: invalid instance head '?'`.
    );
}

render_section_75 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A `struct` or `union` declaration may carry `#derive(Class, ...)` between
        \\the keyword and the body. Each named class must itself declare a derive
        \\macro; the compiler runs that macro at declaration time and splices the
        \\instance it produces into the program.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Point :: struct #derive(Show, PartialEq) { x: s64; y: string; }
        \\
        \\Shape :: union #derive(Show, PartialEq) {
        \\    Dot:    void;
        \\    Line:   s64;
        \\    Label:  string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Point = .{ .x = 1, .y = "left" };
        \\    b: Point = .{ .x = 1, .y = "left" };
        \\    c: Point = .{ .x = 2, .y = "left" };
        \\    print("show   = %\n", show(a));
        \\    print("a == b = %\n", a == b);
        \\    print("a == c = %\n", a == c);
        \\
        \\    s: Shape = Shape.Line(7);
        \\    t: Shape = Shape.Line(7);
        \\    u: Shape = Shape.Dot;
        \\    print("union  = %\n", show(s));
        \\    print("dot    = %\n", show(u));
        \\    print("s == t = %\n", s == t);
        \\    print("s == u = %\n", s == u);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\show   = {1, left}
        \\a == b = 1
        \\a == c = 0
        \\union  = Line(7)
        \\dot    = Dot
        \\s == t = 1
        \\s == u = 0
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\The generated instance is an ordinary instance in every respect. It takes
        \\part in selection on the same terms as a hand-written one, under the rules
        \\in How an Instance Is Chosen, and a second instance declared for the same
        \\type is the ordinary ambiguity rather than an override.
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\A class with no derive macro cannot be derived. Naming one is
        \\`error: typeclass 'Plain' does not declare a derive macro`, reported at
        \\the type declaration, and every later call to the class method fails as
        \\well because no instance was ever created.
    );
    subheading(5, "What the standard classes generate");
    table_header(6, "Class   —   Macro   —   What it writes");
    table_row(7, "`Show`   |   `derive_show`   |   `{f1, f2}` for a struct, `Variant` or `Variant(payload)` for a union.");
    table_row(8, "`PartialEq`   |   `derive_partial_eq`   |   Field-by-field `&&` for a struct; same-variant payload comparison for a union.");
    table_row(9, "`Functor`   |   `derive_functor`   |   `fmap` over the final type parameter, mapping every field or variant that uses it.");
    table_row(10, "`Applicative`   |   `derive_applicative`   |   `pure` and `ap`; one-field structs only, or the single focused union variant.");
    table_row(11, "`Monad`   |   `derive_monad`   |   `bind`, under the same shape restrictions as `Applicative`.");
    table_row(12, "`Error`   |   `derive_error`   |   `report_error` for a non-generic tagged union whose every variant payload is itself `Error`.");
    table_row(13, "`Serialize`, `Deserialize`   |   `derive_serialize`, `derive_deserialize`   |   Field-wise and variant-wise encoders and decoders, in `Serialization`.");
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\`FromString` deliberately ships no derive macro. A parser for a user type
        \\has to decide what its accepted spellings are, and no field-wise rule
        \\produces a useful answer. The `Error` derive and the `#from` union
        \\variants that pair with it are covered in the chapter on `Error`,
        \\`Termination`, and `main`.
    );
    bullet(15, #string,oneline
        \\The `#derive(...)` list on a struct or union is a comma-separated list of
        \\plain identifiers. There is no syntax for passing options to a macro, so
        \\the `Derive_Options` a macro receives is always empty and every macro uses
        \\its default focus.
    );
    bullet(16, #string,oneline
        \\`#derive` is accepted on `struct`, `union`, and `distinct` declarations.
        \\On an `enum` it does not parse: `Color :: enum #derive(Show) { ... }` is
        \\`error: unexpected token 'derive'`.
    );
    bullet(17, #string,oneline
        \\A derive macro that rejects the shape it was handed reports its own
        \\`#compile_error` text, such as `Show derive is not defined for #raw union`
        \\or `Functor derive needs exactly one focused type parameter`.
    );
    note(18, #string,oneline
        \\A derived `Show` on a generic type is only lowered where a call demands
        \\the monomorphization. `print("%", value)` boxes the argument and looks the
        \\shower up by mangled name at code generation, so a generic instance with
        \\no direct `show` call anywhere in the program prints the type name in
        \\braces instead. Write `print("%", show(value))` for generic types.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Tagged :: union #derive(Show) { Some: $A; None: void; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Tagged(s64) = Tagged.Some(9);
        \\    print("through print = %\n", t);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\through print = {Tagged_int}
    );
    subheading(21, "Deriving on a `distinct` type");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`#derive` on a `distinct` declaration is a different feature that shares
        \\the spelling. It generates no code at all. A `distinct` type inherits none
        \\of its representation's operators by default, and the derive list is the
        \\list of operator classes it is permitted to borrow.
    );
    code_block(23, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Meters  :: distinct f64 #derive(Add, Sub, Neg, PartialEq, PartialOrd, Mul(Meters, f64), Show);
        \\Seconds :: distinct f64 #derive,all;
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Meters = 3.0;
        \\    b: Meters = 4.0;
        \\    print("a+b   = %\n", show(a + b));
        \\    print("a-b   = %\n", show(a - b));
        \\    print("-a    = %\n", show(-a));
        \\    print("a*2   = %\n", show(a * 2.0));
        \\    print("a<b   = %\n", a < b);
        \\    print("a==b  = %\n", a == b);
        \\
        \\    s: Seconds = 3.0;
        \\    t: Seconds = 4.0;
        \\    print("s+t   = %\n", show(s + t));
        \\    print("s/t   = %\n", show(s / t));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(24, "text", #string
        \\a+b   = 7
        \\a-b   = -1
        \\-a    = -3
        \\a*2   = 6
        \\a<b   = 1
        \\a==b  = 0
        \\s+t   = 7
        \\s/t   = 0.75
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The operator is resolved on the representation and the nominal tag is put
        \\back only when the representation's result is the representation itself.
        \\`Meters + Meters` is `Meters`, and so is `Meters / Meters`, but
        \\`Meters == Meters` stays `bool`.
    );
    subheading(26, "Naming operand types");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A bare class name in the list derives the same-type instance: every
        \\operand and the result are the nominal type. An entry with type arguments
        \\names the operand types where they differ, which is what makes scaling a
        \\dimensioned quantity expressible.
    );
    table_header(28, "Derive entry   —   Instance it permits");
    table_row(29, "`Add`   |   `Meters + Meters`, yielding `Meters`.");
    table_row(30, "`Mul(Meters, f64)`   |   `Meters * f64`, yielding `Meters`. The mirrored form needs its own `Mul(f64, Meters)`.");
    table_row(31, "`#derive,all`   |   Every operator the representation supports, written instead of a list.");
    code_block(32, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Meters :: distinct f64
        \\    #derive(Add, Show, PartialEq, Mul(Meters, f64), Mul(f64, Meters), Div(Meters, Meters));
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Meters = 3.0;
        \\    b: Meters = 4.0;
        \\    scale: f64 = 2.0;
        \\    print("a * scale = %\n", show(a * scale));
        \\    print("scale * a = %\n", show(scale * a));
        \\    ratio := a / b;
        \\    print("a / b     = %\n", show(ratio));
        \\    print("ratio type= %\n", type_name(type_of(ratio)));
        \\    print("cmp  type = %\n", type_name(type_of(a == b)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(33, "text", #string
        \\a * scale = 6
        \\scale * a = 6
        \\a / b     = 0.75
        \\ratio type= Meters
        \\cmp  type = bool
    );
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\An operator that was not derived names the fix in its diagnostic:
        \\`no operator '*' for Meters and Meters: a distinct type gets only the
        \\classes it derives; add '#derive(...)' or '#derive,all' on the declaration
        \\of 'Meters'`. `#derive` on a `distinct` with neither a list nor `,all` is
        \\`error: '#derive' on a distinct type needs a class list or ',all'`.
    );
    note(35, #string,oneline
        \\The derive list on a `distinct` type gates operators only. A named
        \\typeclass method still resolves through the ordinary generic instances,
        \\because the predicates those instances test are true of the
        \\representation. `Meters :: distinct f64;` with no derive at all still
        \\answers `show(a)` with `3.5`.
    );
}

render_section_76 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A derive macro is an ordinary `#expand` macro, named by the `#derive(...)`
        \\directive on a typeclass declaration. The compiler calls it once per type
        \\that requests the class, and the macro's job is to `#insert` an `instance`
        \\declaration.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The call is always made with the same three parameters: the type itself,
        \\a reflection summary of it, and the options the request carried. The macro
        \\returns nothing; its effect is whatever it inserts.
    );
    code_block(2, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Arity :: typeclass (T: type) #derive(derive_arity) #minimal(arity_of) {
        \\    arity_of :: (value: T) -> s64;
        \\}
        \\
        \\derive_arity :: (T: type, info: Derive_Type_Info, opts: Derive_Options) #expand {
        \\    if !Is_Struct(T) #compile_error "Arity can only be derived for structs";
        \\    empty_focuses: []s64;
        \\    empty_replacements: []Code;
        \\    section    := derive_type_section(info, empty_focuses);
        \\    input_type := derive_type_apply(info, empty_focuses, empty_replacements);
        \\    count      := info.fields.count;
        \\    #insert #code {
        \\        ``section :: instance Arity {
        \\            arity_of :: (value: ``input_type) -> s64 { return ``count; }
        \\        }
        \\    };
        \\}
        \\
        \\Point  :: struct #derive(Arity, Show) { x: s64; y: s64; }
        \\Triple :: struct #derive(Arity) { a: s64; b: s64; c: s64; }
        \\Pair   :: struct #derive(Arity) { k: $K; v: $V; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Point  = .{ .x = 1, .y = 2 };
        \\    t: Triple;
        \\    q: Pair(s64, string) = .{ .k = 1, .v = "x" };
        \\    print("Point  % arity=%\n", show(p), arity_of(p));
        \\    print("Triple arity=%\n", arity_of(t));
        \\    print("Pair   arity=%\n", arity_of(q));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(3, "text", #string
        \\Point  {1, 2} arity=2
        \\Triple arity=3
        \\Pair   arity=2
    );
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Everything the macro computes is spliced into the `#code` block with the
        \\double-backtick escape. `section` and `input_type` are `Code` values built
        \\by the reflection intrinsics, and `count` is an ordinary compile-time
        \\integer; splicing all three is what makes one macro serve `Point`,
        \\`Triple`, and the generic `Pair`. A body whose shape depends on a loop
        \\over the members is usually built as a source string and `#insert`ed
        \\instead, which is what the standard derives do.
    );
    subheading(5, "The reflection types");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\`std/Typeclass/Derive.glo` declares the structures the compiler fills in.
        \\The Prelude imports `Typeclass`, so `Derive_Type_Info`, `Derive_Options`,
        \\`Code`, and the intrinsics below resolve in a program with no import line
        \\at all; the `#import "Typeclass";` commonly written beside a derive macro
        \\is not what makes them visible.
    );
    table_header(7, "Type   —   Member   —   Meaning");
    table_row(8, "`Derive_Type_Info`   |   `base`   |   The ordinary `Type_Info`: tag, canonical name, size, alignment.");
    table_row(9, "`Derive_Type_Info`   |   `params`   |   Generic parameters in declaration order, as `Type_Param_Info`.");
    table_row(10, "`Derive_Type_Info`   |   `fields`   |   Struct fields, as `Derive_Field_Info`. Empty for a union.");
    table_row(11, "`Derive_Type_Info`   |   `variants`   |   Tagged-union variants, as `Derive_Variant_Info`. Empty for a struct.");
    table_row(12, "`Derive_Type_Info`   |   `raw_union`   |   True for a `#raw union`, where tagged-union logic does not apply.");
    table_row(13, "`Type_Param_Info`   |   `name`, `index`   |   Source name and zero-based position; `index` is what the query helpers take.");
    table_row(14, "`Derive_Field_Info`   |   `name`, `ty`   |   Field name and its type resolved in the type's generic scope.");
    table_row(15, "`Derive_Variant_Info`   |   `name`, `payload`   |   Variant name and payload type; a payload-less variant reports `void`.");
    table_row(16, "`Derive_Options`   |   `focus_params`   |   Requested focus parameter names. Always empty in current source.");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Because there is no syntax for passing options in `#derive(...)`, a macro
        \\that calls `derive_focus_params(info, opts, n)` always gets the default:
        \\the final `n` type parameters. A macro with no use for `opts` may leave the
        \\parameter untouched; `derive_error` writes `_ := opts;` to record the
        \\intent, and the other standard derives that ignore it write nothing.
    );
    subheading(18, "The code-building intrinsics");
    table_header(19, "Call   —   Result");
    table_row(20, "`derive_type_info(T)`   |   The `Derive_Type_Info` for `T`. The compiler supplies it to a derive macro already.");
    table_row(21, "`derive_type_section(info, focuses)`   |   The instance head, with focused parameters left as holes: `Pair($K, _)`.");
    table_row(22, "`derive_type_apply(info, focuses, reps)`   |   A concrete type expression, substituting `reps` for the focused parameters.");
    table_row(23, "`derive_type_fn(params, ret)`   |   A function-type expression, for building `F(($A) -> $B)`.");
    table_row(24, "`type_uses_param(T, i)`   |   True when `T` mentions parameter `i` anywhere.");
    table_row(25, "`type_is_param(T, i)`   |   True when `T` is exactly parameter `i`: true for `A`, false for `[]A`.");
    table_row(26, "`type_occurs_covariantly(T, i)`   |   True when every occurrence of parameter `i` in `T` is covariant.");
    table_row(27, "`derive_focus_params(info, opts, n)`   |   The `n` focused parameter indexes, defaulting to the final `n`.");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\The head shape a macro must emit depends on the arity of the class. A
        \\one-parameter class takes the section alone; a two-parameter class such as
        \\`PartialEq(L, R)` takes a tuple head, which for a same-type instance pairs
        \\the section with the applied type.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\
        \\Same_Shape :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) #derive(derive_same_shape) #minimal(same_shape) {
        \\    same_shape :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool;
        \\}
        \\
        \\derive_same_shape :: (T: type, info: Derive_Type_Info, opts: Derive_Options) #expand {
        \\    _ := opts;
        \\    empty_focuses: []s64;
        \\    empty_replacements: []Code;
        \\    section := derive_type_section(info, empty_focuses);
        \\    input_type := derive_type_apply(info, empty_focuses, empty_replacements);
        \\    #insert #code {
        \\        (``section, ``input_type) :: instance Same_Shape {
        \\            same_shape :: (a: ``input_type, b: ``input_type) -> bool {
        \\                return true;
        \\            }
        \\        }
        \\    };
        \\}
        \\
        \\Point :: struct #derive(Same_Shape) { x: s64; y: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Point;
        \\    q: Point;
        \\    print("same = %\n", same_shape(p, q));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\same = 1
    );
    subheading(31, "Reading fields and variants");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\A macro that handles both struct and union shapes tests `Is_Struct` and
        \\`Is_Union`, refuses a `#raw union` explicitly, and walks `info.fields` or
        \\`info.variants`. Both loops run at compile time, so the string they build
        \\is a constant by the time the instance is inserted.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\#import "String_Builder";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\Members :: typeclass (T: type) #derive(derive_members) #minimal(member_names) {
        \\    member_names :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\members_body :: (info: Derive_Type_Info) -> string
        \\    #memory(returns_fresh, reads(info), noescape(info))
        \\{
        \\    source := sb_new();
        \\    sb_append(*source, "return \"");
        \\    first := true;
        \\    for field: info.fields {
        \\        if !first sb_append(*source, ", ");
        \\        sb_append(*source, field.name);
        \\        first = false;
        \\    }
        \\    for variant: info.variants {
        \\        if !first sb_append(*source, ", ");
        \\        sb_append(*source, variant.name);
        \\        if variant.payload != void sb_append(*source, "(_)");
        \\        first = false;
        \\    }
        \\    sb_append(*source, "\";");
        \\    return sb_to_string(source);
        \\}
        \\
        \\derive_members :: (T: type, info: Derive_Type_Info, opts: Derive_Options) #expand {
        \\    if !Is_Struct(T) && !Is_Union(T) {
        \\        #compile_error "Members can only be derived for struct or union types";
        \\    }
        \\    if Is_Union(T) && info.raw_union {
        \\        #compile_error "Members derive is not defined for #raw union";
        \\    }
        \\    _ := opts;
        \\    empty_focuses: []s64;
        \\    empty_replacements: []Code;
        \\    section := derive_type_section(info, empty_focuses);
        \\    input_type := derive_type_apply(info, empty_focuses, empty_replacements);
        \\    #insert #code {
        \\        ``section :: instance Members {
        \\            member_names :: (value: ``input_type) -> string {
        \\                #insert members_body(info);
        \\            }
        \\        }
        \\    };
        \\}
        \\
        \\Point :: struct #derive(Members) { x: s64; y: s64; }
        \\Shape :: union  #derive(Members) { Dot: void; Line: s64; }
        \\Cell  :: struct #derive(Members) { key: $K; value: $V; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    p: Point;
        \\    s: Shape = Shape.Dot;
        \\    c: Cell(s64, string);
        \\    print("Point = %\n", member_names(p));
        \\    print("Shape = %\n", member_names(s));
        \\    print("Cell  = %\n", member_names(c));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\Point = x, y
        \\Shape = Dot, Line(_)
        \\Cell  = key, value
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\Deriving `Members` for `Cell` produces one instance covering every
        \\application, because `derive_type_section` with an empty focus list is the
        \\type name alone and `derive_type_apply` reproduces the declared
        \\parameters.
    );
    subheading(36, "Focused type parameters");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\A class that transforms one type parameter needs the head to leave that
        \\parameter open. `derive_focus_params` chooses which parameter that is,
        \\`derive_type_section` turns it into a hole, and `derive_type_apply`
        \\substitutes a replacement for it in each method signature. For a
        \\two-parameter `Cell(K, V)` focused on the last parameter, the head becomes
        \\`Cell($K, _)`, the input type `Cell(K, $A)`, and the output `Cell(K, B)`.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\#import "Typeclass";
        \\
        \\Map_Last :: typeclass (F: (type) -> type) #derive(derive_map_last) #minimal(map_last) {
        \\    map_last :: (fa: F($A), f: (A) -> $B) -> F(B);
        \\}
        \\
        \\map_last_field :: (field: Derive_Field_Info, focus: s64) -> Code {
        \\    name := field.name;
        \\    if !type_uses_param(field.ty, focus) return #code { .``name = fa.``name };
        \\    if !type_is_param(field.ty, focus) {
        \\        #compile_error "Map_Last handles a direct focused field only";
        \\    }
        \\    return #code { .``name = f(fa.``name) };
        \\}
        \\
        \\derive_map_last :: (F: type, info: Derive_Type_Info, opts: Derive_Options) #expand {
        \\    if !Is_Struct(F) #compile_error "Map_Last can only be derived for structs";
        \\    focuses := derive_focus_params(info, opts, 1);
        \\    if focuses.count != 1 #compile_error "Map_Last needs exactly one focused type parameter";
        \\    focus := focuses[0];
        \\    section     := derive_type_section(info, focuses);
        \\    input_type  := derive_type_apply(info, focuses, .[#code { $A }]);
        \\    output_type := derive_type_apply(info, focuses, .[#code { B }]);
        \\    #insert #code {
        \\        ``section :: instance Map_Last {
        \\            map_last :: (fa: ``input_type, f: (A) -> $B) -> ``output_type {
        \\                return .{
        \\                    #inline for field: info.fields {
        \\                        ``map_last_field(field, focus),
        \\                    }
        \\                };
        \\            }
        \\        }
        \\    };
        \\}
        \\
        \\Cell :: struct #derive(Map_Last) { key: $K; value: $V; }
        \\
        \\length :: (s: string) -> s64 { return s.count; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    c: Cell(string, string) = .{ .key = "id", .value = "hello" };
        \\    d := map_last(c, length);
        \\    print("key = % value = %\n", d.key, d.value);
        \\    print("type = %\n", type_name(type_of(d)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\key = id value = 5
        \\type = Cell_string_int
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\`map_last_field` is a compile-time helper returning `Code`, and the
        \\`#inline for` inside the aggregate literal splices one element per field.
        \\`type_uses_param` distinguishes the field that must be transformed from
        \\the ones that are copied; `type_is_param` refuses a field such as
        \\`[]A` that mentions the parameter but is not it, which is the same
        \\restriction the standard `Functor` derive enforces.
    );
    note(41, #string,oneline
        \\A generated union arm must bind its payload with a pattern capture. Every
        \\standard derive macro emits `case Variant(__glo_payload);` rather than
        \\reading `value.Variant`, because a direct payload read is rejected unless
        \\the tag has been proved. Writing `out.Variant = ...` on the way out is a
        \\variant selection and needs no capture.
    );
    bullet(42, #string,oneline
        \\A macro reports a bad shape with `#compile_error`, and the text appears
        \\verbatim at the failing directive: deriving `Arity` for a union gives
        \\`error: Arity can only be derived for structs`. The method the macro
        \\writes still has to satisfy the class's `#minimal`, and because signature
        \\keys are compared after the head arguments are substituted, a wrong return
        \\type in generated code surfaces as a `#minimal` failure rather than as a
        \\signature mismatch.
    );
}

render_section_77 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Equality is a two-parameter class over the operand types, so the left and
        \\right sides of `==` need not be the same type. Both operators have default
        \\bodies written in terms of each other, and `#minimal` records that
        \\implementing either one is enough.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\PartialEq :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) #derive(derive_partial_eq)
        \\    #minimal('==' | '!=')
        \\{
        \\    '==' :: (left: L, right: R) -> bool #operator(left, 8) #inline {
        \\        return !(left != right);
        \\    }
        \\    '!=' :: (left: L, right: R) -> bool #operator(left, 8) #inline {
        \\        return !(left == right);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\Writing both is permitted and is what the Prelude does for strings, where
        \\each has its own intrinsic. Writing neither is
        \\`error: instance '(Handle, Handle) :: PartialEq' does not satisfy
        \\#minimal('==' | '!=')`, with a note pointing at the `#minimal` directive in
        \\the standard library.
    );
    code_block(3, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Handle :: struct { id: s64; }
        \\Name   :: struct { text: string; }
        \\
        \\(Handle, Handle) :: instance PartialEq {
        \\    '!=' :: (a: Handle, b: Handle) -> bool { return a.id != b.id; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\(Handle, Name) :: instance PartialEq {
        \\    '==' :: (a: Handle, b: Name) -> bool { return b.text == "h"; }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    x: Handle = .{ .id = 1 };
        \\    y: Handle = .{ .id = 1 };
        \\    n: Name   = .{ .text = "h" };
        \\    print("x == y = %\n", x == y);
        \\    print("x != y = %\n", x != y);
        \\    print("x == n = %\n", x == n);
        \\    print("x != n = %\n", x != n);
        \\    print("orderings = % % % %\n",
        \\        cast(s64)Ordering.Less, cast(s64)Ordering.Equal,
        \\        cast(s64)Ordering.Greater, cast(s64)Ordering.Unordered);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(4, "text", #string
        \\x == y = 1
        \\x != y = 0
        \\x == n = 1
        \\x != n = 0
        \\orderings = 0 1 2 3
    );
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\The first instance supplies only `!=` and gets `==` from the default; the
        \\second supplies only `==` for a mixed operand pair and gets `!=`. Because
        \\the instance is keyed on the operand tuple, `(Handle, Name)` and
        \\`(Name, Handle)` are separate instances and only the one that was written
        \\exists.
    );
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\`#derive(PartialEq)` writes a field-by-field conjunction for a struct and
        \\a same-variant payload comparison for a union, which is described in
        \\Deriving Instances with `#derive`. A hand-written instance for the same
        \\operand pair does not replace the derived one: both are declared, and the
        \\first call that has to choose between them is the ambiguity.
    );
    subheading(7, "`Eq` is a marker with a superclass");
    paragraph(8, #string,oneline
        \\`Eq` has no members at all. Its content is entirely in its class-level
        \\`where`, which demands same-type `PartialEq`, and in the meaning attached
        \\to it: an `Eq` type's equality is a real mathematical equality, reflexive
        \\for every value.
    );
    code_block(9, "glosso", #string
        \\Eq :: typeclass (T: type) where PartialEq(T, T) {}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Eq where Is_Integer(T) {}
        \\$T :: instance !Eq where Is_Float(T) {}
        \\bool :: instance Eq {}
        \\$T :: instance Eq where Is_String(T) {}
        \\type :: instance Eq {}
        \\label :: instance Eq {}
        \\void :: instance Eq {}
    );
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\`$T :: instance !Eq where Is_Float(T)` is a negative instance. Floats are
        \\`PartialEq`, because `==` on them is well defined, but they are explicitly
        \\not `Eq`, because a NaN is not equal to itself. A negative instance wins
        \\over any positive one, so no later blanket instance can quietly restore
        \\`Eq` for a float.
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\Marking a user type `Eq` is an empty instance body, and the evidence query
        \\`Eq(T)` is a compile-time constant usable in a `where` clause, an `#if`,
        \\or a top-level constant.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Version :: struct #derive(Show) { major: s64; minor: s64; }
        \\
        \\(Version, Version) :: instance PartialEq {
        \\    '==' :: (a: Version, b: Version) -> bool {
        \\        return a.major == b.major && a.minor == b.minor;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Version :: instance Eq {}
        \\
        \\(Version, Version) :: instance PartialOrd {
        \\    '<=>' :: (a: Version, b: Version) -> Ordering {
        \\        if a.major < b.major return Ordering.Less;
        \\        if a.major > b.major return Ordering.Greater;
        \\        if a.minor < b.minor return Ordering.Less;
        \\        if a.minor > b.minor return Ordering.Greater;
        \\        return Ordering.Equal;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\EQ_VERSION :: Eq(Version);
        \\EQ_F64     :: Eq(f64);
        \\EQ_S64     :: Eq(s64);
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a: Version = .{ .major = 1, .minor = 2 };
        \\    b: Version = .{ .major = 1, .minor = 7 };
        \\    print("a == b  = %  a != b = %\n", a == b, a != b);
        \\    print("a <  b  = %  a <= b = %\n", a < b, a <= b);
        \\    print("a >  b  = %  a >= b = %\n", a > b, a >= b);
        \\    print("a <=> b = %\n", a <=> b);
        \\    print("Eq(Version)=% Eq(f64)=% Eq(s64)=%\n", EQ_VERSION, EQ_F64, EQ_S64);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\a == b  = 0  a != b = 1
        \\a <  b  = 1  a <= b = 1
        \\a >  b  = 0  a >= b = 0
        \\a <=> b = 0
        \\Eq(Version)=1 Eq(f64)=0 Eq(s64)=1
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\A procedure that requires reflexive equality names it in its own `where`.
        \\`find_at :: (values: []$T, wanted: T) -> s64 where Eq(T)` accepts an
        \\`[]s64` or an `[]string` and rejects an `[]f64` with
        \\`error: constraint failed for find_at`. The constraint expresses an intent
        \\that `PartialEq` alone cannot: a lookup that must find back the value it
        \\was given.
    );
    note(15, #string,oneline
        \\`where Eq(T)` alone does not license `==` inside the body. `Eq` has no
        \\members, and the body needs the class that owns the operator. The compiler
        \\warns rather than failing:
        \\`warning: no '==' for <T>: the where-clause only provides Eq`, with
        \\`note: reported at the definition; add the class to the 'where' clause`.
        \\Write `where PartialEq(T, T)`, adding `Eq(T)` beside it when reflexivity
        \\is the real requirement.
    );
    subheading(16, "`PartialOrd` and `<=>`");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Ordering is also a two-parameter class. One member has no default; the
        \\four comparison operators are all defined in terms of it, so an instance
        \\that supplies `<=>` acquires `<`, `<=`, `>`, and `>=` unchanged.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\PartialOrd :: typeclass (L: type, R: type) {
        \\    '<=>' :: (a: L, b: R) -> Ordering #operator(left, 10);
        \\
        \\    '<' :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool #operator(left, 10) #inline {
        \\        c := a <=> b;
        \\        return c == Ordering.Less;
        \\    }
        \\    '<=' :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool #operator(left, 10) #inline {
        \\        c := a <=> b;
        \\        return c == Ordering.Less || c == Ordering.Equal;
        \\    }
        \\    '>' :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool #operator(left, 10) #inline {
        \\        c := a <=> b;
        \\        return c == Ordering.Greater;
        \\    }
        \\    '>=' :: (a: L, b: R) -> bool #operator(left, 10) #inline {
        \\        c := a <=> b;
        \\        return c == Ordering.Greater || c == Ordering.Equal;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\`PartialOrd` has no `#minimal` directive, so the implicit minimal applies:
        \\every member without a default body must be implemented, which here is
        \\`<=>` alone. Overriding one of the four defaults is allowed and is
        \\occasionally worth doing when the type can answer `<` more cheaply than it
        \\can answer a full three-way comparison.
    );
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\All five operators sit at precedence tier 10, above the equality operators
        \\at tier 8. The whole scale is set out in Operator Precedence and the 0-20
        \\Scale.
    );
    subheading(21, "`Ordering` and what `Unordered` means");
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`Ordering` is a plain enum with four variants, in this order:
        \\`Less`, `Equal`, `Greater`, `Unordered`. `show` on an enum yields the
        \\ordinal rather than the variant name, which is why `a <=> b` printed `0`
        \\above rather than `Less`.
    );
    table_header(23, "Variant   —   Meaning");
    table_row(24, "`Less`   |   The left operand precedes the right.");
    table_row(25, "`Equal`   |   Neither precedes the other and they compare the same.");
    table_row(26, "`Greater`   |   The left operand follows the right.");
    table_row(27, "`Unordered`   |   The two operands are not comparable at all.");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\`Unordered` is the reason the class is `PartialOrd` rather than a total
        \\`Ord`. Floating point produces it for any comparison involving a NaN. The
        \\four defaulted operators all answer `false` for an `Unordered` result, so
        \\`a < b`, `a > b`, and `a == b` can all be false at once.
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\Every comparison-driven container and algorithm in the standard library
        \\treats `Unordered` as a failure rather than as an ordering. Sorting,
        \\`BinaryHeap`, `BTreeMap`, and `BTreeSet` all detect it and report an
        \\error instead of proceeding, because a sort or a search tree built on an
        \\inconsistent comparison has no correct answer to give.
    );
    code_block(30, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Sort";
        \\#import "Math";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    good: [4]f64 = .[3.0, 1.0, 2.0, 0.5];
        \\    r1 := sort(good[..]);
        \\    if r1 == {
        \\        case .Ok;         print("sorted = % % % %\n", good[0], good[1], good[2], good[3]);
        \\        case .Err(error); print("sort err = %\n", sort_error_reason(error));
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    bad: [3]f64 = .[1.0, quiet_nan(), 2.0];
        \\    r2 := sort(bad[..]);
        \\    if r2 == {
        \\        case .Ok;         print("nan sort ok\n");
        \\        case .Err(error); print("nan sort err = %\n", sort_error_reason(error));
        \\    }
        \\
        \\    cmp := 1.0 <=> quiet_nan();
        \\    print("1.0 <=> nan is Unordered = %\n", cmp == Ordering.Unordered);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\sorted = 0.5 1 2 3
        \\nan sort err = comparison produced an unordered value
        \\1.0 <=> nan is Unordered = 1
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\The two error enums carry a variant for it: `Sort_Error.Unordered` and
        \\`Container_Error.Unordered`, both reporting `comparison produced an
        \\unordered value`. The container operations preflight the comparisons they
        \\will need and leave the structure logically unchanged; a sort may already
        \\have rearranged part of the slice by the time it detects the problem.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\`void` has hand-written `PartialEq` and `PartialOrd` instances that answer
        \\`true` and `Ordering.Equal` unconditionally. One inhabitant means one
        \\answer, so neither is a comparison of stored bits.
    );
    bullet(34, #string,oneline
        \\`?T` carries a hand-written `PartialEq` guarded by `where PartialEq(T, T)`
        \\rather than a derived one, because the built-in option type cannot carry a
        \\`#derive` directive. Two absences are equal, an absence and a presence
        \\never are, and two presences defer to the payload.
    );
    bullet(35, #string,oneline
        \\A `distinct` type inherits neither class automatically. `PartialEq` and
        \\`PartialOrd` have to appear in its `#derive` list, and the result of a
        \\comparison is `bool` rather than the nominal type.
    );
}

render_section_78 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`Show` is the protocol every formatting call goes through. A `%`
        \\placeholder in `print`, `format`, `tprint` or `format_args` consumes
        \\one argument and replaces itself with `show` of that argument, so a
        \\type becomes printable by acquiring a `Show` instance and by nothing
        \\else. `FromString` is the parsing counterpart, and the two are
        \\deliberately not symmetric: `Show` ships a derive macro, `FromString`
        \\does not.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\Show :: typeclass (T: type) #derive(derive_show) {
        \\    show :: (value: T) -> string #memory unknown;
        \\}
        \\
        \\FromString :: typeclass (T: type) {
        \\    from_string :: (#empty: T, text: string) -> ?T
        \\        #memory(unknown, reads(text), noescape(text));
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\Both live in `std/Prelude/Show_and_Read.glo`, which every program
        \\loads unqualified, so neither name needs an import. `show` returns a
        \\`string` and declares `#memory unknown`, which leaves each instance
        \\free to return a borrow, a static, or a fresh allocation. The leading
        \\`#empty:` parameter of `from_string` carries the result type without
        \\becoming a runtime argument, which is why the type is written in the
        \\call: `from_string(s64, "-42")`.
    );
    subheading(3, "The Prelude instances");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\The Prelude keys most of its `Show` instances on a predicate rather
        \\than on a type, so a whole family is covered by one instance. A
        \\program may still add a concrete instance for any of these types; a
        \\concrete head outranks a blanket one, and there is no orphan rule.
    );
    table_header(5, "Instance head   —   Renders as   —   Implementation");
    table_row(6, "`string`, `string16`   |   the text itself   |   borrow; `string16` is decoded to UTF-8");
    table_row(7, "`$T where Is_Integer(T)`   |   decimal digits   |   `#magic \"show_int\"`");
    table_row(8, "`$T where Is_Float(T)`   |   decimal, `1.5`   |   `#magic \"show_float\"`");
    table_row(9, "`$T where Is_Bool(T)`   |   `0` or `1`   |   `#magic \"show_bool\"`");
    table_row(10, "`$T where Is_Enum(T)`   |   the ordinal, not the name   |   `#magic \"show_enum\"`");
    table_row(11, "`$T where Is_Pointer(T) || Is_Procedure(T)`   |   the address   |   `#magic \"show_pointer\"`");
    table_row(12, "`type`   |   the canonical type name   |   `#magic \"type_name\"`");
    table_row(13, "`any`   |   the boxed value   |   `#magic \"show_any\"`");
    table_row(14, "`label`   |   the label   |   `#magic \"show_label\"`");
    table_row(15, "`void`   |   `.()`   |   hand written");
    table_row(16, "`?$T where Show(T)`   |   `Some(payload)` or `None`   |   hand written, `?T` cannot carry `#derive`");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\`FromString` has four Prelude instances: `string`, and blanket
        \\instances guarded by `Is_Bool(T)`, `Is_Integer(T)` and `Is_Float(T)`.
        \\The integer parser is overflow checked at every width including 128
        \\bits, and the float parser accepts `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`, exponents
        \\and subnormals. All of them consume the whole string and answer
        \\`.None` on any leftover byte.
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\There is no `Show` instance for `E!T`. Passing a result to `print`
        \\is `error: variadic arg type `Result_int_string` does not implement
        \\`Show` in call `print``; match on it and show the payload instead.
        \\`?T` does have one, so an option prints directly.
    );
    subheading(19, "What `#derive(Show)` produces");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\`derive_show` accepts a struct or a tagged union. A struct renders as
        \\its fields in declaration order inside braces; a union renders as the
        \\variant name, with the payload in parentheses when the variant has
        \\one. The generated body builds the text with a `String_Builder` in
        \\the temporary allocator, and it reads a union payload through a
        \\pattern capture rather than a field access, because a payload may
        \\only be bound by a match. A `#raw` union is `error: Show derive is
        \\not defined for #raw union`, since a raw union has no tag to
        \\dispatch on.
    );
    code_block(21, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\Money :: struct { cents: s64; }
        \\
        \\Money :: instance Show {
        \\    show :: (value: Money) -> string {
        \\        return tprint("$%.%", value.cents / 100, value.cents % 100);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Point :: struct #derive(Show) { x: s64; name: string; }
        \\
        \\Tag :: union #derive(Show) { Plain: void; Count: s64; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    m: Money = .{ .cents = 1234 };
        \\    p: Point = .{ .x = 1, .name = "origin" };
        \\    t: Tag = .Count(9);
        \\    u: Tag = .Plain;
        \\    print("money = %\n", m);
        \\    print("point = %\n", p);
        \\    print("tag   = % and %\n", t, u);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(22, "text", #string
        \\money = $12.34
        \\point = {1, origin}
        \\tag   = Count(9) and Plain
    );
    subheading(23, "`args: ...Show` and the `%` loop");
    paragraph(24, #string,oneline
        \\Every formatting entry point has the same shape: a `#comptime fmt:
        \\string` followed by `args: ...Show`. The constraint on the pack is
        \\what rejects an unprintable argument at the call site rather than
        \\inside the formatter. Constrained variadics are covered in
        \\Typeclass-Constrained Variadics.
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The formatter itself is ordinary Glosso, in `__format` in
        \\`std/Format.glo`. It walks the format string one byte at a time. A
        \\`%` appends `show(args[ai])` and advances the argument index; every
        \\other byte is copied. Nothing else in the format string is
        \\interpreted: there are no width, precision, or conversion fields, and
        \\a type controls its own rendering entirely through its instance.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\Because `fmt` is a `#comptime` parameter, the placeholder count is
        \\checked while the call is checked. Too few arguments is `error:
        \\print: format has 3 '%' placeholder(s) but 1 argument(s) given`, and
        \\too many is the same message with the numbers reversed. The runtime
        \\fallbacks in `__format` for a surplus `%` are therefore unreachable
        \\through `print`.
    );
    subheading(27, "Writing a literal percent");
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\A percent sign that is not a placeholder is written `\%`. The
        \\formatter recognises the two-byte sequence and emits a single `%`,
        \\and the compile-time placeholder count skips it, so an escaped
        \\percent consumes no argument.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("% of % is %\n", 50, 200, 100);
        \\    print("50\% of 200 is 100\n");
        \\    print("progress: %\% done\n", 40);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\50 of 200 is 100
        \\50% of 200 is 100
        \\progress: 40% done
    );
    subheading(31, "The `Format` helpers");
    table_header(32, "Call   —   Result   —   Allocator");
    table_row(33, "`print(fmt, args)`   |   written to standard output   |   temporary; `print` formats through `tprint`");
    table_row(34, "`format(fmt, args)`   |   a fresh `string`   |   `context.allocator`");
    table_row(35, "`tprint(fmt, args)`   |   a temporary `string`   |   `context.temp_allocator`");
    table_row(36, "`format_args(fmt, args, .allocator = a)`   |   a fresh `string`   |   the named argument, defaulting to `context.allocator`");
    table_row(37, "`format_int(value, base)`   |   digits in `base`   |   fresh; usable in a compile-time expression");
    table_row(38, "`format_int_with_allocator(value, base, a)`   |   digits in `base`   |   the explicit allocator");
    code_block(39, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\#import "Context";
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    heap := format("% and %", 1, "two");
        \\    temp := tprint("% and %", 3, "four");
        \\    args := format_args("%-%", 5, 6, .allocator = context.temp_allocator);
        \\    print("format     = %\n", heap);
        \\    print("tprint     = %\n", temp);
        \\    print("format_args= %\n", args);
        \\    print("hex        = %\n", format_int(255, 16));
        \\    print("binary     = %\n", format_int(10, 2));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(40, "text", #string
        \\format     = 1 and two
        \\tprint     = 3 and four
        \\format_args= 5-6
        \\hex        = ff
        \\binary     = 1010
    );
    subheading(41, "Writing a `FromString` instance");
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\`FromString` carries no derive macro on purpose: a parser has to
        \\decide what it accepts, and no field-by-field expansion can guess
        \\that. Asking for one is `error: typeclass 'FromString' does not
        \\declare a derive macro`. An instance returns `?T`, answers `.None`
        \\for anything it does not fully consume, and is free to call
        \\`from_string` for its components.
    );
    code_block(43, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\Celsius :: struct { degrees: s64; }
        \\
        \\Celsius :: instance Show {
        \\    show :: (value: Celsius) -> string {
        \\        return tprint("%C", value.degrees);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Celsius :: instance FromString {
        \\    from_string :: (#empty: Celsius, text: string) -> ?Celsius {
        \\        if text.count == 0 return .None;
        \\        if text[text.count - 1] != #char "C" return .None;
        \\        digits := text[0..text.count - 1];
        \\        n := from_string(s64, digits);
        \\        if n == {
        \\            case .Some(v); return .Some(.{ .degrees = v });
        \\            case .None;    return .None;
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := from_string(Celsius, "21C");
        \\    b := from_string(Celsius, "21F");
        \\    if a == { case .Some(v); print("parsed  = %\n", v); case .None; print("parsed  = none\n"); }
        \\    if b == { case .Some(v); print("refused = %\n", v); case .None; print("refused = none\n"); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(44, "text", #string
        \\parsed  = 21C
        \\refused = none
    );
    note(45, #string,oneline
        \\A `%` placeholder boxes its argument into `any`, and the backend then
        \\looks for a lowered `show` for that exact type. A concrete instance
        \\always has one. A generic instance only produces one where some call
        \\site demanded that monomorphisation, so a value whose only `Show`
        \\instance is generic prints as its type name in braces. Writing
        \\`print("%", show(x))` forces the instantiation and is the reliable
        \\form for any generic carrier, `?T` included.
    );
    code_block(46, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Tagged :: union #derive(Show) { Some_Value: $A; Nothing: void; }
        \\
        \\Color :: enum { Red; Green; Blue; }
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    t: Tagged(s64) = .Some_Value(9);
        \\    o: ?s64 = .Some(3);
        \\    print("print only  = %\n", t);
        \\    print("option only = %\n", o);
        \\    print("enum        = %\n", Color.Green);
        \\    print("bool        = %\n", true);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(47, "text", #string
        \\print only  = {Tagged_int}
        \\option only = {Option_int}
        \\enum        = 1
        \\bool        = 1
    );
    note(48, #string,oneline
        \\The same program with `show(t)` and `show(o)` written anywhere prints
        \\`Some_Value(9)` and `Some(3)`. Enums and booleans render as ordinals
        \\in every case: `show_enum` is the integer shower, so `Color.Green` is
        \\`1` and `show(true)` is `1`. A variant name in output requires a
        \\hand-written instance.
    );
}

render_section_79 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Postfix `?` is not built into the compiler's notion of errors. It is
        \\an operator owned by the `Try` typeclass, and any type that
        \\implements `Try` is a carrier that `?` can unwrap. The class splits a
        \\carrier into two associated types, an `Output` for the value the
        \\happy path produces and a `Residual` for the token the failing path
        \\carries, and gives three methods that convert between them.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\Propagate :: typeclass (From: type, To: type) {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: To, value: From) -> To;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Try_Branch :: union {
        \\    Continue: $T;
        \\    Break: $R;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Try :: typeclass (Carrier: type)
        \\    #minimal(from_output, from_residual, branch) {
        \\    Output :: type;
        \\    Residual :: type;
        \\
        \\    from_output :: (#empty: Carrier, output: Output) -> Carrier;
        \\    from_residual :: (#empty: Carrier, residual: Residual) -> Carrier;
        \\    branch :: (value: Carrier) -> Try_Branch(Output, Residual);
        \\
        \\    '?' :: (value: Carrier) -> Try_Branch(Output, Residual) #operator(suffix) {
        \\        return branch(value);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\`std/Typeclass/Try.glo` is the whole declaration. The `?` operator
        \\has a default body in the class, so no instance ever writes `'?'`;
        \\implementing `branch` is what makes `?` work, and an instance that
        \\declares `'?'` with any other signature is `error: instance method
        \\'?' does not match any declaration in typeclass 'Try'`. `#minimal`
        \\names the three methods, and the two associated types are required
        \\implicitly, because a missing `Output` makes the signature of
        \\`from_output` unresolvable.
    );
    table_header(3, "Member   —   Direction   —   Role");
    table_row(4, "`Output`   |   associated type   |   what a successful carrier holds");
    table_row(5, "`Residual`   |   associated type   |   what a failed carrier holds, stripped of the carrier");
    table_row(6, "`from_output`   |   value into carrier   |   wraps a bare `Output` returned from a Try-returning function");
    table_row(7, "`from_residual`   |   residual into carrier   |   builds the carrier that `?` returns early");
    table_row(8, "`branch`   |   carrier into token   |   the only method an instance must think about");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\`Try_Branch(T, R)` is an ordinary two-variant union, not a compiler
        \\type. `branch` returns `.Continue(value)` or `.Break(residual)`, and
        \\that token is the entire interface between a carrier and the `?`
        \\machinery. A carrier need not be a union itself; a struct carrier
        \\that decides in `branch` which side it is on works exactly as well.
    );
    subheading(10, "How `x?` is checked");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\`?` needs a boundary that can absorb a `Break`. That is either an
        \\enclosing function whose return type implements `Try`, or an enclosing
        \\`#try` block. Without one the operator is `error: postfix '?' requires
        \\a Try-returning function or an enclosing #try block`. With one, the
        \\checker performs these steps.
    );
    bullet(12, #string,oneline
        \\The operand type must implement `Try`. A type with no instance at
        \\all is `error: no suffix operator '?' for int`, because `?` is
        \\resolved as an ordinary suffix operator before any Try-specific
        \\reasoning happens.
    );
    bullet(13, #string,oneline
        \\`Output` and `Residual` are projected from `Try(operand)`, and
        \\`branch(operand)` is called. The result must be exactly
        \\`Try_Branch(Output, Residual)`; an instance that returns something
        \\else is rejected at the use site, for example `error: Try propagation
        \\operator returned int, expected Try_Branch_int_X_Residual from
        \\Try.branch`.
    );
    bullet(14, #string,oneline
        \\On `.Continue(v)` the expression's value is `v`, typed as the
        \\operand's `Output`.
    );
    bullet(15, #string,oneline
        \\On `.Break(r)`, if the boundary's `Residual` differs from the
        \\operand's, `propagate` is inserted to convert one into the other.
        \\That conversion is the subject of `Propagate` and Multi-Error
        \\Programs.
    );
    bullet(16, #string,oneline
        \\`from_residual` on the boundary's carrier turns the converted
        \\residual into the value the function returns, and control leaves the
        \\boundary.
    );
    bullet(17, #string,oneline
        \\Separately, returning a bare `Output` from a Try-returning function
        \\wraps it with `from_output`, which is why `return x + y;` is legal in
        \\a function declared `E!s64`.
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\Each of those pieces is callable by hand, so the expansion can be
        \\written out and compared. The two functions below behave identically.
    );
    code_block(19, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\fetch :: (ok: bool) -> string!s64 {
        \\    if !ok return .Err("bad input");
        \\    return .Ok(20);
        \\}
        \\
        \\sugared :: (ok: bool) -> string!s64 {
        \\    v := fetch(ok)?;
        \\    return v + 1;
        \\}
        \\
        \\expanded :: (ok: bool) -> string!s64 {
        \\    v: s64;
        \\    token := branch(fetch(ok));
        \\    if token == {
        \\        case .Continue(output); v = output;
        \\        case .Break(residual);  return from_residual(string!s64, residual);
        \\    }
        \\    return from_output(string!s64, v + 1);
        \\}
        \\
        \\report :: (r: string!s64) -> string {
        \\    if r == {
        \\        case .Ok(v);  return show(v);
        \\        case .Err(e); return e;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("sugared  % %\n", report(sugared(true)), report(sugared(false)));
        \\    print("expanded % %\n", report(expanded(true)), report(expanded(false)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(20, "text", #string
        \\sugared  21 bad input
        \\expanded 21 bad input
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\The `#empty:` witness in `from_output(string!s64, v + 1)` is why the
        \\carrier type is written as an argument. `Try(C).Output` and
        \\`Try(C).Residual` are also usable as ordinary type annotations, which
        \\is how a generic helper names the two halves of a carrier it was
        \\handed.
    );
    subheading(22, "The built-in carriers");
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\`?T` and `E!T` are compiler-built-in types, but their `Try` instances
        \\are ordinary Glosso in `std/Prelude/Option.glo` and
        \\`std/Prelude/Result.glo`. Each residual is a separate type that
        \\carries the failure without the carrier around it, which is what lets
        \\one function's failure be converted into another's.
    );
    table_header(24, "Carrier   —   Output   —   Residual");
    table_row(25, "`?T`   |   `T`   |   `Option_Residual`, an enum with the single variant `None`");
    table_row(26, "`E!T`   |   `T`   |   `Result_Residual(E)`, a struct holding one `error: E`");
    code_block(27, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Parse_Error :: struct #derive(Show) { text: string; }
        \\
        \\parse :: (text: string) -> Parse_Error!s64 {
        \\    v := from_string(s64, text);
        \\    if v == {
        \\        case .Some(n); return .Ok(n);
        \\        case .None;    return .Err(.{ .text = text });
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\sum_two :: (a: string, b: string) -> Parse_Error!s64 {
        \\    x := parse(a)?;
        \\    y := parse(b)?;
        \\    return x + y;
        \\}
        \\
        \\head :: (xs: []s64) -> ?s64 {
        \\    if xs.count == 0 return .None;
        \\    return .Some(xs[0]);
        \\}
        \\
        \\first_plus_one :: (xs: []s64) -> ?s64 {
        \\    return head(xs)? + 1;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    r := sum_two("3", "4");
        \\    e := sum_two("3", "oops");
        \\    if r == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(x); print("err %\n", show(x)); }
        \\    if e == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(x); print("err %\n", show(x)); }
        \\
        \\    storage: [1]s64;
        \\    storage[0] = 41;
        \\    empty: []s64;
        \\    print("some %\n", show(first_plus_one(storage[..])));
        \\    print("none %\n", show(first_plus_one(empty)));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(28, "text", #string
        \\ok  7
        \\err {oops}
        \\some Some(42)
        \\none None
    );
    subheading(29, "`#try` blocks");
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\`#try { expr }` introduces a boundary without introducing a function.
        \\It is an expression whose value is the carrier, so a `?` inside it
        \\stops at the closing brace instead of leaving the enclosing function.
        \\The carrier type is inferred from the first `?` in the block; a block
        \\with none is `error: cannot infer the carrier type of #try expression;
        \\propagate at least one value with '?'`.
    );
    code_block(31, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Parse_Error :: struct #derive(Show) { text: string; }
        \\
        \\parse :: (text: string) -> Parse_Error!s64 {
        \\    v := from_string(s64, text);
        \\    if v == {
        \\        case .Some(n); return .Ok(n);
        \\        case .None;    return .Err(.{ .text = text });
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    good := #try { parse("5")? + parse("6")? };
        \\    bad  := #try { parse("5")? + parse("x")? };
        \\    if good == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\    if bad  == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(32, "text", #string
        \\ok  11
        \\err {x}
    );
    paragraph(33, #string,oneline
        \\The grammar is narrow on purpose, because the block has to have a
        \\value. It holds exactly one expression and no statements: a trailing
        \\semicolon is `error: #try contains one expression; remove the
        \\semicolon`, and a `return` inside it is `error: return is not allowed
        \\inside a #try expression`. The expression's type must be the
        \\carrier's `Output`. Error-only cleanup at a boundary is written with
        \\`#try defer`, described in `defer` and `#try defer`.
    );
    subheading(34, "Writing a `Try` instance");
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\An instance supplies the two associated types and the three methods.
        \\`branch` carries the real logic; `from_output` and `from_residual`
        \\are usually one constructor each. Because the head may be generic,
        \\a carrier parameterised by its payload is written with a section
        \\hole and referred to without the `$` inside the body.
    );
    code_block(36, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Validated :: union { Valid: $T; Invalid: string; }
        \\Invalid_Reason :: struct { message: string; }
        \\
        \\Validated($T) :: instance Try {
        \\    Output   :: T;
        \\    Residual :: Invalid_Reason;
        \\
        \\    from_output :: (#empty: Validated(T), output: T) -> Validated(T) {
        \\        return .Valid(output);
        \\    }
        \\    from_residual :: (#empty: Validated(T), residual: Invalid_Reason) -> Validated(T) {
        \\        return .Invalid(residual.message);
        \\    }
        \\    branch :: (value: Validated(T)) -> Try_Branch(T, Invalid_Reason) {
        \\        if value == {
        \\            case .Valid(v);   return .Continue(v);
        \\            case .Invalid(m); return .Break(.{ .message = m });
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\positive :: (n: s64) -> Validated(s64) {
        \\    if n <= 0 return .Invalid("not positive");
        \\    return .Valid(n);
        \\}
        \\
        \\doubled :: (n: s64) -> Validated(s64) {
        \\    v := positive(n)?;
        \\    return v * 2;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := doubled(7);
        \\    b := doubled(-1);
        \\    if a == { case .Valid(v); print("valid   %\n", v); case .Invalid(m); print("invalid %\n", m); }
        \\    if b == { case .Valid(v); print("valid   %\n", v); case .Invalid(m); print("invalid %\n", m); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(37, "text", #string
        \\valid   14
        \\invalid not positive
    );
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\`return v * 2;` in `doubled` is a bare `Output`, wrapped by
        \\`from_output`. The `Residual` is a type of the instance's own
        \\choosing, and keeping it distinct from the carrier is what allows a
        \\`Propagate` instance to convert it later.
    );
    note(39, #string,oneline
        \\A missing associated type does not report itself as a missing
        \\associated type. Dropping `Output` makes the declared signature of
        \\`from_output` unresolvable, and the instance is rejected as `error:
        \\instance 'X :: Try' does not satisfy #minimal(from_output,
        \\from_residual, branch)` with a note pointing at the class. Check the
        \\associated types first when that message appears.
    );
}

render_section_80 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`Propagate` is the conversion `?` performs when the value being
        \\propagated fails with one residual and the enclosing boundary
        \\requires another. It is deliberately narrower than a general implicit
        \\conversion protocol: nothing else in the language consults it, and it
        \\is never applied to a successful value.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\Propagate :: typeclass (From: type, To: type) {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: To, value: From) -> To;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\The class has two parameters and one method, and the head is written
        \\as a tuple: `(From_Type, To_Type) :: instance Propagate`. The leading
        \\`#empty:` witness names the target, which is what lets the compiler
        \\select a conversion by its destination rather than by inference from
        \\the return type. `?` inserts a `propagate` call only when the two
        \\residual types differ; a function propagating its own error type
        \\needs no instance at all.
    );
    subheading(3, "The Prelude's residual lifting");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\A residual is not the error; for `E!T` it is `Result_Residual(E)`,
        \\a struct wrapping one `E`. Writing conversions at the residual level
        \\would mean writing wrapper structs by hand for every pair of error
        \\types. The Prelude removes that step with one blanket instance in
        \\`std/Prelude/Result.glo`.
    );
    code_block(5, "glosso", #string
        \\(Result_Residual($From), Result_Residual($To)) :: instance Propagate
        \\    where Propagate(From, To) {
        \\    propagate :: (
        \\        #empty: Result_Residual(To),
        \\        value: Result_Residual(From),
        \\    ) -> Result_Residual(To) {
        \\        return .{ .error = propagate(To, value.error) };
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\The consequence is the rule to remember: a `Propagate` instance
        \\between two error types is enough to make `?` widen between the
        \\corresponding results. The instance below converts `Low_Error` into
        \\`High_Error`, and `?` in a `High_Error!s64` function then accepts a
        \\`Low_Error!s64` with no other change.
    );
    code_block(7, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Low_Error  :: struct #derive(Show) { code: s64; }
        \\High_Error :: struct #derive(Show) { code: s64; note: string; }
        \\
        \\(Low_Error, High_Error) :: instance Propagate {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: High_Error, value: Low_Error) -> High_Error {
        \\        return .{ .code = value.code, .note = "lifted" };
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\low :: (ok: bool) -> Low_Error!s64 {
        \\    if !ok return .Err(.{ .code = 7 });
        \\    return .Ok(2);
        \\}
        \\
        \\high :: (ok: bool) -> High_Error!s64 {
        \\    v := low(ok)?;
        \\    return v * 10;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := high(true);
        \\    b := high(false);
        \\    if a == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\    if b == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(8, "text", #string
        \\ok  20
        \\err {7, lifted}
    );
    subheading(9, "Instances between residuals");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The lifting instance only covers result-to-result. Converting between
        \\different carriers means writing the instance at the residual level,
        \\because there is no error type on the option side to lift from.
        \\Absorbing an option into a result is the common case: the residual
        \\`Option_Residual` carries no information, so the instance supplies
        \\the error it stands for.
    );
    code_block(11, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Missing :: struct #derive(Show) { key: string; }
        \\
        \\(Option_Residual, Result_Residual(Missing)) :: instance Propagate {
        \\    propagate :: (
        \\        #empty: Result_Residual(Missing),
        \\        value: Option_Residual,
        \\    ) -> Result_Residual(Missing) {
        \\        return .{ .error = .{ .key = "unknown" } };
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\lookup :: (present: bool) -> ?s64 {
        \\    if !present return .None;
        \\    return .Some(9);
        \\}
        \\
        \\required :: (present: bool) -> Missing!s64 {
        \\    return lookup(present)? + 1;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := required(true);
        \\    b := required(false);
        \\    if a == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\    if b == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(12, "text", #string
        \\ok  10
        \\err {unknown}
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\The opposite direction, discarding an error to reach an option, is
        \\not in the Prelude either, and for a stronger reason: throwing
        \\information away is a decision a program makes, not a default. One
        \\generic instance over every error type makes `?` usable from a `?T`
        \\function.
    );
    code_block(14, "glosso", #string
        \\(Result_Residual($E), Option_Residual) :: instance Propagate {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: Option_Residual, value: Result_Residual(E)) -> Option_Residual {
        \\        return .None;
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(15, #string,oneline
        \\Without one of these the call site names the missing conversion by
        \\listing what it looked for: `error: no overload 'propagate' accepts
        \\these arguments`, followed by the two candidates
        \\`propagate :: (__empty0: Result_Residual(To), value:
        \\Result_Residual(From)) -> Result_Residual(To)` and
        \\`propagate :: (__empty0: To, value: From) -> To`. The first is the
        \\lifting instance, the second the class requirement.
    );
    subheading(16, "Widening across a library boundary");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\Because instance selection is global and there is no orphan rule, the
        \\widening instance belongs to whichever code owns the wider error, not
        \\to the library that raised the narrow one. A library can therefore
        \\define a small, precise error type and stay unaware of every
        \\application that folds it into a larger one.
    );
    code_block(18, "glosso", #string
        \\// config.glo
        \\Config_Error :: struct #derive(Show) { key: string; }
        \\
        \\lookup :: (key: string) -> Config_Error!string {
        \\    if key == "port" return .Ok("8080");
        \\    return .Err(.{ .key = key });
        \\}
    );
    subheading(19, "`#from` variants");
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\Writing one `Propagate` instance per contributing error is
        \\mechanical, so a union variant may be marked `#from`. The compiler
        \\then generates the instance from the source type to the union. The
        \\application below folds a library error and a local one into a single
        \\`Boot_Error` and propagates both with plain `?`.
    );
    code_block(21, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#load "config.glo";
        \\
        \\Bad_Number :: struct #derive(Show) { text: string; }
        \\
        \\Boot_Error :: union #derive(Show) {
        \\    #from config: Config_Error;
        \\    #from number: Bad_Number;
        \\}
        \\
        \\to_number :: (text: string) -> Bad_Number!s64 {
        \\    v := from_string(s64, text);
        \\    if v == {
        \\        case .Some(n); return .Ok(n);
        \\        case .None;    return .Err(.{ .text = text });
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\port :: (key: string) -> Boot_Error!s64 {
        \\    text := lookup(key)?;
        \\    return to_number(text)?;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    a := port("port");
        \\    b := port("host");
        \\    if a == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\    if b == { case .Ok(v); print("ok  %\n", v); case .Err(e); print("err %\n", show(e)); }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(22, "text", #string
        \\ok  8080
        \\err config({host})
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\`#from` is independent of `#derive(Error)`; it is a property of the
        \\variant, and the generated instance is exactly what a hand-written
        \\one would be. Writing the two instances below in place of the two
        \\`#from` markers produces the same program.
    );
    code_block(24, "glosso", #string
        \\(Config_Error, Boot_Error) :: instance Propagate {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: Boot_Error, value: Config_Error) -> Boot_Error {
        \\        return .config(value);
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\(Bad_Number, Boot_Error) :: instance Propagate {
        \\    propagate :: (#empty: Boot_Error, value: Bad_Number) -> Boot_Error {
        \\        return .number(value);
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\The generator is narrow, because each restriction would otherwise
        \\produce an instance that cannot be keyed on a single source type.
    );
    table_header(26, "Rejected form   —   Diagnostic");
    table_row(27, "a `#raw` union   |   `#from is not defined for #raw union`");
    table_row(28, "a generic union   |   `#from does not yet support generic unions`");
    table_row(29, "a payload-less variant   |   `#from variant 'a' must have a payload`");
    table_row(30, "two variants of one type   |   `#from variant 'b' duplicates the source type A_Error`");
    note(31, #string,oneline
        \\`Propagate` is consulted only by `?`. Returning `.Err(e)` explicitly
        \\from a function whose error type differs is an ordinary type
        \\mismatch, and no conversion is attempted. This keeps the widening
        \\visible: it happens exactly where a `?` is written.
    );
}

render_section_81 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\`Error` and `Termination` are the two classes at the edge of a
        \\program. `Error` says how a value explains itself to the operator;
        \\`Termination` says how a value becomes a process exit status. `main`
        \\may return any type that implements `Termination`, and the standard
        \\pairing of the two is what makes `main :: () -> App_Error!void` a
        \\complete error-reporting program.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\Error :: typeclass (E: type) #derive(derive_error) #minimal(report_error) {
        \\    report_error :: (error: E) -> void;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Termination :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(termination_status) {
        \\    termination_status :: (value: T) -> s32;
        \\}
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\`report_error` returns nothing and is expected to write a diagnostic,
        \\conventionally to standard error. The class does not fix a format,
        \\a destination, or a severity; it fixes only that an error type knows
        \\how to describe itself without being handed a formatter.
    );
    subheading(3, "Allocation-free reporting");
    paragraph(4, #string,oneline
        \\Reporting is the last thing a failing program does, and it must work
        \\when the allocator is exhausted and when the higher-level output
        \\modules are themselves the thing that failed, since their own error
        \\types implement `Error`. `std/Typeclass/Error.glo` therefore provides
        \\two helpers that write straight to file descriptor 2 through the
        \\`file_write_result` intrinsic, touching neither the allocator nor
        \\`Io`.
    );
    code_block(5, "glosso", #string
        \\error_report_write :: (text: string) {
        \\    written, code := __error_file_write_result(2, text);
        \\    _ := written;
        \\    _ := code;
        \\}
        \\
        \\error_report_line :: (category: string, message: string) {
        \\    error_report_write(category);
        \\    error_report_write(": ");
        \\    error_report_write(message);
        \\    error_report_write("\n");
        \\}
    );
    table_header(6, "Helper   —   Writes");
    table_row(7, "`error_report_write(text)`   |   `text`, exactly, with no newline");
    table_row(8, "`error_report_line(category, message)`   |   `category`, `\": \"`, `message`, newline");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Both ignore the write result. A report that cannot be written has
        \\nowhere left to report that fact, so the failure is dropped rather
        \\than escalated. An instance that wants formatted output can still
        \\call `tprint`, at the cost of depending on the temporary allocator on
        \\the failure path.
    );
    subheading(10, "`derive_error`");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\`#derive(Error)` on a tagged union writes the obvious instance: match
        \\the union, and call `report_error` on the payload. It composes with
        \\`#from`, so one union declaration can both aggregate several error
        \\types and report all of them.
    );
    table_header(12, "Requirement   —   Diagnostic when violated");
    table_row(13, "must be a tagged union   |   `Error can currently be derived only for tagged unions`");
    table_row(14, "not `#raw`   |   `Error derive is not defined for #raw union`");
    table_row(15, "not generic   |   `Error derive does not yet support generic unions`");
    table_row(16, "every variant has a payload   |   `Error derive requires every union variant to have an Error payload`");
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\A payload that exists but has no `Error` instance is not caught by
        \\that last check. The generated body fails to compile instead, with
        \\`error: no overload 'report_error' accepts these arguments` pointing
        \\into `std/Typeclass/Error.glo`, which is the line the macro emitted.
    );
    subheading(18, "`Termination` and the return type of `main`");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\`prepare_main_termination_dispatch` checks `main`'s return type at
        \\compile time and inserts the `termination_status` call. Two instances
        \\ship with the standard library: one for every integer type, and one
        \\for `E!void` where `E` implements `Error`.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\$T :: instance Termination where Is_Integer(T) {
        \\    termination_status :: (value: T) -> s32 {
        \\        return cast(s32)value;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\$E!void :: instance Termination where Error(E) {
        \\    termination_status :: (value: E!void) -> s32 {
        \\        if value == {
        \\            case .Ok; return 0;
        \\            case .Err(error);
        \\                report_error(error);
        \\                return 1;
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    table_header(21, "`main` returns   —   Status   —   Notes");
    table_row(22, "nothing   |   0   |   the ordinary form");
    table_row(23, "any integer type   |   the value, narrowed to `s32`   |   the host applies its own exit-code rules");
    table_row(24, "`E!void` where `E: Error`   |   0 on `.Ok`, 1 on `.Err`   |   `report_error(error)` runs first");
    table_row(25, "a type with a `Termination` instance   |   whatever `termination_status` returns   |   a struct or enum works; see below");
    table_row(26, "`?T`, `bool`, `E!T` for non-`void` `T`   |   rejected   |   `main return type Option_int does not implement Termination`");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\The `E!void` instance is the only one keyed on a result, and it is
        \\keyed on `void` specifically: `main :: () -> My_Err!s64` is `error:
        \\main return type Result_int_My_Err does not implement Termination`,
        \\because a successful `s64` has no meaning at the process boundary
        \\that the integer instance does not already give. `bool` is rejected
        \\for the same reason `Is_Integer(bool)` is false.
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\An application-specific instance is a small declaration and needs
        \\nothing else. Its `termination_status` must have a runtime body: a
        \\`#magic` implementation is `main's Termination implementation must
        \\have a runtime function body`.
    );
    code_block(29, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Exit_Report :: struct { failures: s64; }
        \\
        \\Exit_Report :: instance Termination {
        \\    termination_status :: (value: Exit_Report) -> s32 {
        \\        if value.failures == 0 return 0;
        \\        return 3;
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () -> Exit_Report {
        \\    print("two tests failed\n");
        \\    return .{ .failures = 2 };
        \\}
    );
    code_block(30, "text", #string
        \\two tests failed
        \\glosso: program exited with status exit code: 3
    );
    subheading(31, "A complete failing program");
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\Putting the pieces together: each leaf error implements `Error`, a
        \\union aggregates them with `#from` and derives `Error`, and `main`
        \\returns that union in a result. Every `?` widens through the
        \\generated `Propagate` instances, the union's derived `report_error`
        \\dispatches to the payload's, and the `E!void` `Termination` instance
        \\turns the whole thing into status 1.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\Disk_Full :: struct { path: string; bytes: s64; }
        \\Disk_Full :: instance Error {
        \\    report_error :: (error: Disk_Full) -> void {
        \\        error_report_write("disk full writing ");
        \\        error_report_write(error.path);
        \\        error_report_write("\n");
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Timed_Out :: struct { seconds: s64; }
        \\Timed_Out :: instance Error {
        \\    report_error :: (error: Timed_Out) -> void {
        \\        error_report_line("timed out", "the request took too long");
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\Job_Error :: union #derive(Error) {
        \\    #from disk: Disk_Full;
        \\    #from timeout: Timed_Out;
        \\}
        \\
        \\run :: (which: s64) -> Job_Error!void {
        \\    if which == 0 return .Err(.disk(.{ .path = "out.bin", .bytes = 4096 }));
        \\    return .Err(.timeout(.{ .seconds = 30 }));
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () -> Job_Error!void {
        \\    print("starting\n");
        \\    run(1)?;
        \\    return .Ok();
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\starting
        \\timed out: the request took too long
        \\glosso: program exited with status exit code: 1
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\Returning `.Ok()` produces status 0 and prints nothing extra. The
        \\`glosso:` line is the driver reporting a nonzero status under
        \\`--run`; the exit status itself is the subject of Environment,
        \\Toolchain, and Exit Codes.
    );
    note(36, #string,oneline
        \\`Error` and `Termination` are separate on purpose. An error type
        \\reports; a termination type decides a number. A program that wants
        \\several distinct nonzero statuses writes its own `Termination`
        \\instance over its error union rather than trying to encode the
        \\status in `report_error`, whose return type is `void`.
    );
}

render_section_82 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\A variadic parameter may name a typeclass instead of a type. `args:
        \\...Show` accepts any number of arguments provided every one of them
        \\implements `Show`. The constraint is checked element by element at
        \\the call site, so a rejected argument is reported at the argument
        \\that caused it rather than inside the callee. The other variadic
        \\forms are covered in Variadic Parameters.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\show_all         :: (values: ...Show)                { }
        \\count_both       :: (values: ...(Show && PartialEq)) { }
        \\anything         :: (values: ...any)                 { }
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\A parenthesised `&&` list requires every named class of every
        \\element. There is no `||` form; `...(Show || PartialEq)` is `error:
        \\unexpected token '||'`, because a disjunction would leave the callee
        \\with no method it could call on all elements. `...any` is the
        \\unconstrained pack, useful when the body only reflects over the
        \\element types.
    );
    code_block(3, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Format";
        \\
        \\Named :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(name_of) {
        \\    name_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\$T :: instance Named where Is_Integer(T) { name_of :: (v: T) -> string { return "int"; } }
        \\$T :: instance Named where Is_String(T)  { name_of :: (v: T) -> string { return "text"; } }
        \\
        \\join_names :: (args: ...Named) -> string {
        \\    out := "";
        \\    for args {
        \\        if it_index > 0 out = tprint("%,%", out, name_of(it));
        \\        else out = name_of(it);
        \\    }
        \\    return out;
        \\}
        \\
        \\count_both :: (values: ...(Show && PartialEq)) -> s64 {
        \\    return values.count;
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("names = %\n", join_names(1, "x", 2));
        \\    print("both  = %\n", count_both(10, "y"));
        \\}
    );
    code_block(4, "text", #string
        \\names = int,text,int
        \\both  = 2
    );
    subheading(5, "Element-wise checking");
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\A failing element is named with its type, the class it lacks, and the
        \\call it appeared in: passing a `string` to `join_names` above with no
        \\string instance in scope is `error: variadic arg type `string` does
        \\not implement `Named` in call `join_names``. A conjunction reports
        \\the first class the element misses, not the whole list, so an
        \\argument that is `Show` but not `PartialEq` reports only
        \\`does not implement `PartialEq``.
    );
    paragraph(7, #string,oneline
        \\Expansion is checked the same way. `xs...` splices a typed array
        \\into the pack, and because every element has one type the check
        \\runs once for that type: `error: expanded variadic element type
        \\`Plain` does not implement `Show` in call `print``. This is the
        \\form `print` takes when the arguments are already collected.
    );
    code_block(8, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\show_all :: (values: ...Show) {
        \\    for values print("  [%] %\n", it_index, it);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    fixed: [3]s32;
        \\    fixed[0] = 10; fixed[1] = 20; fixed[2] = 30;
        \\    show_all(fixed...);
        \\    show_all(1, "two", 3.5);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(9, "text", #string
        \\  [0] 10
        \\  [1] 20
        \\  [2] 30
        \\  [0] 1
        \\  [1] two
        \\  [2] 3.5
    );
    subheading(10, "The pack inside the callee");
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\The pack is a heterogeneous, read-only view. `type_of` on the pack
        \\itself answers `tuple`; each element is boxed so it can be indexed
        \\with a runtime index, which is what makes the `[]any` view real and
        \\what `__format` relies on when it walks `args[ai]`. Indexing with a
        \\constant, or iterating, recovers the element's precise static type.
    );
    code_block(12, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\inspect :: (values: ...Show) {
        \\    pack_type :: type_of(values);
        \\    elem_type :: type_of(values[1]);
        \\    print("pack type = %\n", pack_type);
        \\    print("elem type = %\n", elem_type);
        \\    print("count     = %\n", values.count);
        \\    print("values[1] = %\n", values[1]);
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    inspect(1, "two", 3.5);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(13, "text", #string
        \\pack type = tuple
        \\elem type = string
        \\count     = 3
        \\values[1] = two
    );
    bullet(14, #string,oneline
        \\`values.count` is the number of arguments, but it is an ordinary
        \\runtime value inside the callee. Binding it with `::` or testing it
        \\in a `#if` is `error: unknown const values`; a body that must branch
        \\on arity does so with `#inline for` instead.
    );
    bullet(15, #string,oneline
        \\A plain `for` over the pack is unrolled, so `it` has the element's
        \\own static type in each iteration and `name_of(it)` dispatches to the
        \\right instance. `it_index` is the position.
    );
    bullet(16, #string,oneline
        \\A runtime index is allowed and reads through the boxed view, so a
        \\`while i < values.count` loop over `values[i]` renders each element
        \\correctly even though the index is not a constant.
    );
    bullet(17, #string,oneline
        \\The view is read-only. Assigning to a pack element is not supported
        \\and currently fails during lowering with `error: internal error:
        \\'op_lbrrbreq_tuple_int_int' is called but was never lowered`; copy
        \\the element into a local instead.
    );
    subheading(18, "`#inline for` and per-element evidence");
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\`#inline for` over a pack is the tool for code that must differ per
        \\element. The body is expanded once per argument with the element's
        \\type known, so `type_of(it)` can be bound to a compile-time constant
        \\and that constant used in an evidence query. `#if Class(T)` answers
        \\whether `T` has an instance, and only the taken branch is checked.
    );
    code_block(20, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\#import "Meta";
        \\
        \\Unit :: typeclass (T: type) #minimal(unit_of) {
        \\    unit_of :: (value: T) -> string;
        \\}
        \\
        \\Meters  :: struct { n: f64; }
        \\Seconds :: struct { n: f64; }
        \\
        \\Meters  :: instance Unit { unit_of :: (value: Meters)  -> string { return "m"; } }
        \\Seconds :: instance Unit { unit_of :: (value: Seconds) -> string { return "s"; } }
        \\Meters  :: instance Show { show :: (value: Meters)  -> string { return "meters"; } }
        \\
        \\label_all :: (values: ...Unit) {
        \\    #inline for values {
        \\        elem :: type_of(it);
        \\        #if Show(elem) {
        \\            print("  % (%) shows as %\n", elem, unit_of(it), Show(elem).show(it));
        \\        } else {
        \\            print("  % (%) has no Show\n", elem, unit_of(it));
        \\        }
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    m: Meters  = .{ .n = 3.0 };
        \\    s: Seconds = .{ .n = 4.0 };
        \\    label_all(m, s);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\  Meters (m) shows as meters
        \\  Seconds (s) has no Show
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\`Show(elem).show` in that body is a scoped typeclass method
        \\reference: it names the `show` of the instance for exactly `elem`,
        \\which is what lets the call be written in a branch that only some
        \\elements reach. The unqualified `unit_of(it)` needs no scope because
        \\the constraint on the pack already guarantees an instance.
    );
    paragraph(23, #string,oneline
        \\Structural conditions rather than class membership are written with
        \\`#if !#meaningful { it.field; } continue;`, which skips an element
        \\whose shape the body cannot use. That form belongs to Structural
        \\Constraints with `#meaningful`.
    );
    note(24, #string,oneline
        \\An evidence query is compile-time only. `Class(T)` in a runtime
        \\expression is `error: internal error: 'Marker' is called but was
        \\never lowered`; bind it to a constant or use it directly in `#if`.
        \\The expression form also takes exactly one type argument:
        \\`Convert(s64, string)` for a two-parameter class is `error:
        \\Convert(T) expects one type argument`, and the one-argument form
        \\answers `false` for such a class rather than reporting anything. A
        \\multi-parameter class can only be queried usefully inside a `where`.
    );
}
